/* Quiz questions for each flashcard set. Written for this site from the specification content,
   in our own words: not copied from Physics & Maths Tutor or Pearson.
   "UNIT/deck-slug": [[question, answer], ...] */
const DECK_QUIZ = {
"WPH11/mechanics":[
["What is the difference between a scalar and a vector? Give one example of each.","A scalar has magnitude only (e.g. speed, mass, energy). A vector has magnitude and direction (e.g. velocity, force, displacement)."],
["What do the gradient and the area under a velocity–time graph represent?","Gradient = acceleration. Area under the graph = displacement."],
["Write the four equations of uniformly accelerated motion (suvat).","v = u + at;  s = ½(u + v)t;  s = ut + ½at²;  v² = u² + 2as. They only apply when acceleration is constant."],
["A ball is thrown horizontally from a cliff. Describe its horizontal and vertical motion (ignore air resistance).","Horizontal: constant velocity, because no horizontal force acts. Vertical: constant downward acceleration g (9.81 m s⁻²), starting from zero vertical velocity. The two motions are independent."],
["State Newton’s second law in terms of momentum.","The resultant force on an object equals its rate of change of momentum (F = Δp/Δt), in the direction of the force. For constant mass this gives F = ma."],
["What conditions must a pair of forces meet to be a Newton’s third law pair?","Equal in magnitude, opposite in direction, the same type of force, acting on two different objects, along the same line."],
["State the principle of conservation of linear momentum.","The total momentum of a system stays constant provided no resultant external force acts on it."],
["Define the moment of a force and state the principle of moments.","Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force. For an object in equilibrium, the sum of clockwise moments equals the sum of anticlockwise moments about any point."],
["Define work done and power, with equations.","Work done = force × distance moved in the direction of the force (W = Fs cos θ). Power = rate of doing work or transferring energy (P = W/t, and P = Fv for a constant force)."],
["How do you calculate efficiency?","Efficiency = useful energy (or power) output ÷ total energy (or power) input, often × 100%."]],
"WPH11/materials":[
["Define density and give its SI unit.","Density = mass ÷ volume (ρ = m/V). Unit: kg m⁻³."],
["What is upthrust, and what is it equal to?","The upward force on an object in a fluid. It equals the weight of the fluid the object displaces (Archimedes’ principle)."],
["State Stokes’ law and the conditions under which it applies.","Viscous drag F = 6πηrv. It applies to a small sphere moving slowly through a fluid with laminar flow."],
["What forces act on a sphere falling at terminal velocity through a liquid, and how are they related?","Weight acts down; upthrust and viscous drag act up. At terminal velocity the resultant force is zero: weight = upthrust + drag."],
["How does temperature affect the viscosity of liquids and of gases?","Liquids become less viscous as temperature rises. Gases become more viscous as temperature rises."],
["State Hooke’s law.","Force is directly proportional to extension (F = kΔx), up to the limit of proportionality."],
["Define stress, strain and the Young modulus.","Stress = force ÷ cross-sectional area (Pa). Strain = extension ÷ original length (no unit). Young modulus = stress ÷ strain, in the linear region (Pa)."],
["What is the difference between elastic and plastic deformation?","Elastic: the material returns to its original shape when the force is removed. Plastic: it stays permanently deformed."],
["How do you find the elastic strain energy from a force–extension graph?","It is the area under the graph. For a material obeying Hooke’s law, E = ½FΔx = ½kΔx²."],
["What is meant by the yield point and by breaking stress?","Yield point: the stress beyond which the material stretches a lot for little or no extra force (plastic flow begins). Breaking stress: the stress at which the material fractures."]],
"WPH12/waves-and-particle-nature-of-light":[
["What is the difference between a transverse and a longitudinal wave? Give an example of each.","Transverse: oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer (e.g. light, waves on a string). Longitudinal: oscillations are parallel to it (e.g. sound)."],
["Write the wave equation and define each quantity.","v = fλ. v is wave speed (m s⁻¹), f is frequency (Hz), λ is wavelength (m)."],
["What does it mean for two sources to be coherent?","They have the same frequency and a constant phase difference."],
["State the condition for constructive and for destructive interference in terms of path difference (sources in phase).","Constructive: path difference = nλ (whole number of wavelengths). Destructive: path difference = (n + ½)λ."],
["How is a stationary wave formed, and what are nodes and antinodes?","Two waves of the same frequency travelling in opposite directions superpose. Nodes are points of zero amplitude; antinodes are points of maximum amplitude."],
["State the equation relating refractive index to the angles of incidence and refraction, and the condition for total internal reflection.","n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂. Total internal reflection needs light travelling from a denser to a less dense medium at an angle greater than the critical angle (sin C = 1/n)."],
["What is diffraction, and when is it most noticeable?","The spreading of a wave when it passes through a gap or around an obstacle. It is greatest when the gap width is similar to the wavelength."],
["Write the diffraction grating equation and define the terms.","nλ = d sin θ. n is the order, λ the wavelength, d the slit spacing, θ the angle of that order from the straight-through direction."],
["What is the photoelectric effect and what is the threshold frequency?","Electrons are emitted from a metal surface when light of high enough frequency shines on it. The threshold frequency is the minimum frequency that releases electrons."],
["Write Einstein’s photoelectric equation and explain each term.","hf = φ + ½mv²max. hf is the photon energy, φ the work function (minimum energy to free an electron), ½mv²max the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electron."],
["Why does the photoelectric effect support the particle model of light?","Emission depends on frequency, not intensity, and starts instantly. One photon gives all its energy to one electron, which the wave model cannot explain."],
["What evidence shows that electrons behave as waves, and what is the de Broglie relation?","Electron diffraction through a crystal or thin graphite gives a ring pattern. λ = h/p, where p is momentum."]],
"WPH12/electric-circuits":[
["Define electric current and potential difference.","Current = rate of flow of charge (I = ΔQ/Δt). Potential difference = energy transferred per unit charge between two points (V = W/Q)."],
["State Ohm’s law.","The current through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it, provided the temperature and other physical conditions stay constant."],
["Write the transport equation for current and define the terms.","I = nqvA. n is the number of charge carriers per unit volume, q the charge on each, v the drift velocity, A the cross-sectional area."],
["Define resistivity and give its unit.","ρ = RA/l, the resistance of a sample of unit length and unit cross-sectional area. Unit: Ω m."],
["How do you combine resistors in series and in parallel?","Series: R = R₁ + R₂ + …  Parallel: 1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + …"],
["State Kirchhoff’s two circuit rules and what each conserves.","Sum of currents into a junction = sum of currents out (conservation of charge). Sum of e.m.f.s round a closed loop = sum of potential differences (conservation of energy)."],
["What is the difference between e.m.f. and terminal potential difference?","E.m.f. is the energy given to each coulomb by the source. Terminal p.d. is the energy per coulomb delivered to the external circuit; it is smaller because of the p.d. across the internal resistance (V = ε − Ir)."],
["How does the resistance of a metal and of a thermistor (NTC) change as temperature rises, and why?","Metal: resistance rises, because lattice ions vibrate more and electrons collide more often. Thermistor: resistance falls, because more charge carriers are released."],
["What does a potential divider do? Give the equation for the output.","It splits a supply p.d. in the ratio of two resistances. Vout = Vin × R₂/(R₁ + R₂), taken across R₂."],
["Give three equations for electrical power.","P = VI, P = I²R, P = V²/R. Energy transferred W = VIt."]],
"WPH14/further-mechanics":[
["Define impulse and state how it relates to momentum.","Impulse = force × time for which it acts (FΔt). It equals the change in momentum."],
["What is the difference between an elastic and an inelastic collision?","Momentum is conserved in both. Kinetic energy is conserved only in an elastic collision; in an inelastic collision some kinetic energy is transferred to other forms."],
["How do you apply conservation of momentum to a collision in two dimensions?","Resolve the momenta into two perpendicular directions and conserve momentum separately in each direction."],
["Write the relationship between kinetic energy and momentum for a non-relativistic particle.","Ek = p²/2m."],
["Define angular velocity and give its relation to linear speed and to period.","Angular velocity ω = angle turned per unit time (rad s⁻¹). v = ωr and ω = 2π/T."],
["Why does an object moving in a circle at constant speed have an acceleration?","Its direction, and so its velocity, is always changing. The acceleration is directed towards the centre of the circle."],
["Write the equations for centripetal acceleration and centripetal force.","a = v²/r = rω².  F = mv²/r = mrω²."],
["Is centripetal force a separate force? Explain.","No. It is the resultant force towards the centre, provided by real forces such as tension, friction, gravity or a normal contact force."],
["What is a radian?","The angle at the centre of a circle subtended by an arc equal in length to the radius. 2π rad = 360°."]],
"WPH14/electric-and-magnetic-fields":[
["Define electric field strength.","The force per unit positive charge acting on a small charge at that point: E = F/Q (N C⁻¹ or V m⁻¹)."],
["State Coulomb’s law.","The force between two point charges is proportional to the product of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of their separation: F = Q₁Q₂/4πε₀r²."],
["What is the field strength between two parallel plates, and what is special about it?","E = V/d. The field is uniform: same strength and direction everywhere between the plates."],
["Define capacitance and give its unit.","Charge stored per unit potential difference: C = Q/V. Unit: farad (F)."],
["Write the equations for the energy stored in a capacitor.","W = ½QV = ½CV² = Q²/2C. It is the area under a p.d.–charge graph."],
["What is the time constant of a capacitor–resistor circuit and what does it tell you?","τ = RC. It is the time for the charge (or current or p.d.) to fall to 1/e, about 37%, of its initial value during discharge."],
["Write the equation for a capacitor discharging through a resistor.","Q = Q₀e^(−t/RC). The same form applies to current and p.d."],
["Define magnetic flux density and give the force on a current-carrying wire in a field.","Flux density B is the force per unit current per unit length on a wire at right angles to the field (tesla). F = BIl sin θ."],
["What is the force on a charge moving in a magnetic field, and what path does it follow when moving at right angles to the field?","F = Bqv sin θ. The force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so the particle moves in a circle (r = p/Bq)."],
["State Faraday’s law and Lenz’s law.","Faraday: the induced e.m.f. is proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux linkage. Lenz: the induced e.m.f. acts in the direction that opposes the change producing it. ε = −d(NΦ)/dt."],
["Define magnetic flux and flux linkage.","Flux Φ = BA (field perpendicular to area), in webers. Flux linkage = NΦ for a coil of N turns."]],
"WPH14/nuclear-and-particle-physics":[
["What did the alpha-particle scattering experiment show about the atom?","Most alpha particles passed straight through, so the atom is mostly empty space. A few were deflected through large angles, so there is a tiny, dense, positively charged nucleus."],
["What is thermionic emission?","The release of electrons from the surface of a heated metal."],
["How does a linac accelerate particles, and why do the drift tubes get longer?","An alternating p.d. accelerates the particles across the gaps between tubes. The tubes get longer so that the faster particles still spend the same time in each tube and stay in step with the alternating supply."],
["Why does a cyclotron need a magnetic field and an alternating electric field?","The magnetic field bends the particles into a circular path. The alternating electric field accelerates them each time they cross the gap between the dees."],
["Why are high energies needed to investigate the structure of nucleons?","High energy means high momentum and so a small de Broglie wavelength (λ = h/p), which is needed to resolve very small structures."],
["What are the two families of hadrons and what are they made of?","Baryons are made of three quarks (e.g. proton uud, neutron udd). Mesons are made of a quark and an antiquark."],
["What is a lepton? Give examples.","A fundamental particle that does not feel the strong force, e.g. electron, muon, and their neutrinos."],
["Which quantities are conserved in particle interactions?","Charge, baryon number and lepton number, as well as energy and momentum."],
["What happens in pair production and in annihilation?","Pair production: a photon’s energy creates a particle and its antiparticle. Annihilation: a particle meets its antiparticle and their mass is converted into photons. Both follow ΔE = c²Δm."],
["How is a particle’s antiparticle related to it?","It has the same mass but opposite charge (and opposite baryon or lepton number)."]],
"WPH15/thermodynamics":[
["Define specific heat capacity and give the equation.","The energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 K, with no change of state. ΔE = mcΔθ."],
["Define specific latent heat and give the equation.","The energy needed to change the state of 1 kg of a substance with no change in temperature. ΔE = LΔm."],
["What is meant by the internal energy of a body?","The sum of the randomly distributed kinetic and potential energies of its molecules."],
["What is absolute zero?","0 K (−273 °C), the temperature at which the molecules of an ideal gas would have zero kinetic energy; the lowest possible temperature."],
["Write the ideal gas equation and define the terms.","pV = NkT. p is pressure, V volume, N the number of molecules, k the Boltzmann constant, T the temperature in kelvin."],
["How is the average kinetic energy of a gas molecule related to temperature?","½m⟨c²⟩ = (3/2)kT. Average kinetic energy is directly proportional to absolute temperature."],
["Write the kinetic theory equation for the pressure of an ideal gas.","pV = ⅓Nm⟨c²⟩, where ⟨c²⟩ is the mean square speed."],
["Explain, using kinetic theory, why a gas exerts a pressure on its container.","Molecules collide with the walls and their momentum changes. The walls exert a force on the molecules, so the molecules exert an equal and opposite force on the walls. Pressure = total force ÷ area."],
["What is a black body radiator?","An ideal object that absorbs all the radiation that falls on it and emits radiation over a continuous range of wavelengths that depends only on its temperature."]],
"WPH15/nuclear-decay":[
["Compare the ionising power and penetration of alpha, beta and gamma radiation.","Alpha: most ionising, stopped by paper or a few cm of air. Beta: moderately ionising, stopped by a few mm of aluminium. Gamma: least ionising, only reduced by thick lead or concrete."],
["Why is radioactive decay described as random and spontaneous?","Random: you cannot predict which nucleus will decay next or when. Spontaneous: it is not affected by external conditions such as temperature or pressure."],
["Define activity and the decay constant.","Activity is the number of decays per second (Bq). The decay constant λ is the probability that a nucleus decays per unit time. A = λN."],
["Define half-life and give its relation to the decay constant.","The time taken for half the unstable nuclei in a sample (or the activity) to decay. t½ = ln 2 / λ."],
["Write the equation for the number of undecayed nuclei after time t.","N = N₀e^(−λt). The same form applies to activity."],
["What is background radiation and how do you allow for it?","Radiation always present from sources such as rocks, cosmic rays and medical uses. Measure the background count and subtract it from each reading."],
["What is nuclear binding energy and how is it linked to mass deficit?","The energy needed to separate a nucleus into its individual nucleons. It equals the mass deficit × c² (ΔE = c²Δm)."],
["What are nuclear fission and nuclear fusion?","Fission: a heavy nucleus splits into two lighter nuclei. Fusion: two light nuclei join to make a heavier one. Both release energy because the products have a higher binding energy per nucleon."],
["Why does fusion need very high temperatures and densities?","Nuclei are positively charged and repel. They need very high kinetic energy to get close enough for the strong force to act, and high density for a high collision rate."]],
"WPH15/astrophysics-and-cosmology":[
["Define gravitational field strength.","The force per unit mass on a small mass at that point: g = F/m (N kg⁻¹)."],
["State Newton’s law of gravitation.","The force between two point masses is proportional to the product of the masses and inversely proportional to the square of their separation: F = Gm₁m₂/r²."],
["Give one similarity and one difference between gravitational and electric fields.","Similarity: both follow an inverse square law for point sources. Difference: gravity is always attractive, but electric forces can attract or repel."],
["State the Stefan–Boltzmann law.","The luminosity of a black body is L = σAT⁴: proportional to surface area and to the fourth power of absolute temperature."],
["State Wien’s law.","λmax T = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m K. The peak wavelength is inversely proportional to the surface temperature, so hotter stars peak at shorter wavelengths."],
["What is a standard candle and why is it useful?","An object of known luminosity. Measuring its intensity on Earth gives its distance using I = L/4πd²."],
["How is trigonometric parallax used to find the distance to a nearby star?","The star’s apparent position against distant stars is measured six months apart. The parallax angle and the radius of Earth’s orbit give the distance."],
["What is plotted on a Hertzsprung–Russell diagram, and where are the main groups of stars?","Luminosity against surface temperature (temperature decreasing to the right). Main sequence runs diagonally; red giants are top right; white dwarfs bottom left."],
["What is red shift and what does it tell us about distant galaxies?","An increase in observed wavelength because the source is moving away. z = Δλ/λ ≈ v/c. Distant galaxies are receding, so the universe is expanding."],
["State Hubble’s law and how it gives an age for the universe.","Recession velocity is proportional to distance: v = H₀d. The age of the universe is about 1/H₀."],
["Why is dark matter thought to exist?","Galaxies rotate faster than their visible mass can explain, so there must be extra mass that does not emit light."]],
"WPH15/oscillations":[
["State the conditions for simple harmonic motion.","The acceleration (or resultant force) is directly proportional to the displacement from equilibrium and always directed towards the equilibrium position: a = −ω²x."],
["Write the equations for displacement and maximum speed in SHM.","x = A cos ωt. Maximum speed vmax = Aω; maximum acceleration amax = Aω²."],
["Give the period of a mass–spring system and of a simple pendulum.","Mass–spring: T = 2π√(m/k). Pendulum: T = 2π√(l/g), for small angles."],
["Describe the energy changes during one oscillation of an undamped system.","Energy moves between kinetic (maximum at equilibrium) and potential (maximum at the amplitude). The total energy stays constant."],
["What is damping?","The loss of energy from an oscillating system because of resistive forces, which makes the amplitude decrease."],
["What is the difference between free and forced oscillations?","Free: the system oscillates at its natural frequency with no external driving force. Forced: a periodic driving force makes it oscillate at the driving frequency."],
["What is resonance?","When the driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the system, energy transfer is most efficient and the amplitude is a maximum."],
["How does damping affect a resonance curve?","More damping gives a lower, broader peak, and the peak moves to a slightly lower frequency."],
["Give one example where resonance is useful and one where it is a problem.","Useful: microwave ovens, MRI, tuning a radio. Problem: bridges or buildings oscillating in wind or earthquakes."]],
"WCH11/formulae-equations-and-amount-of-substance":[
["Define the mole and state the value of the Avogadro constant.","One mole contains 6.02 × 10²³ particles (the Avogadro constant, L). It is the amount of substance with the same number of particles as there are atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12."],
["What is the difference between an empirical formula and a molecular formula?","Empirical: the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms of each element in a compound. Molecular: the actual number of atoms of each element in one molecule."],
["Give the three equations linking moles to mass, to solution concentration and to gas volume at r.t.p.","n = mass ÷ molar mass;  n = concentration × volume (dm³);  n = volume ÷ 24 dm³ (at room temperature and pressure)."],
["Write the ideal gas equation with the units needed.","pV = nRT, with p in Pa, V in m³, T in K and R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹."],
["How do you calculate percentage yield and atom economy?","Percentage yield = actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100. Atom economy = molar mass of desired product ÷ total molar mass of all products × 100."],
["Give two reasons why a percentage yield is usually below 100%.","Any two of: the reaction is incomplete or reversible; side reactions occur; product is lost in transfers or purification."],
["What is an ionic equation, and what are spectator ions?","An equation showing only the ions and particles that actually react. Spectator ions are present but unchanged, so they are left out."],
["Define relative atomic mass.","The weighted mean mass of an atom of an element compared with 1/12 of the mass of an atom of carbon-12."],
["What is a limiting reagent?","The reactant that is completely used up first; it decides the maximum amount of product."]],
"WCH11/atomic-structure-and-the-periodic-table":[
["Give the relative mass and charge of a proton, neutron and electron.","Proton: mass 1, charge +1. Neutron: mass 1, charge 0. Electron: mass 1/1840 (negligible), charge −1."],
["What are isotopes?","Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons (so different mass numbers)."],
["What are the main stages in a mass spectrometer, and what is it used for?","The sample is vaporised and ionised, the ions are accelerated, separated according to mass-to-charge ratio and detected. It gives isotopic masses and abundances, and the relative molecular mass from the molecular ion peak."],
["Define first ionisation energy and write an equation for sodium.","The energy needed to remove one electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous atoms to form one mole of gaseous 1+ ions. Na(g) → Na⁺(g) + e⁻."],
["Which three factors affect ionisation energy?","Nuclear charge, distance of the outer electron from the nucleus (atomic radius), and shielding by inner electrons."],
["Why does first ionisation energy generally increase across a period?","Nuclear charge increases while shielding stays about the same and atomic radius decreases, so the outer electron is held more strongly."],
["Explain the small drops in first ionisation energy between Groups 2 and 3, and between Groups 5 and 6.","Group 3: the outer electron is in a p sub-shell, which is higher in energy and slightly shielded by the s electrons. Group 6: the electron removed is from a paired p orbital, and repulsion between the pair makes it easier to remove."],
["What is an orbital, and how many electrons can s, p and d sub-shells hold?","A region around the nucleus that can hold up to two electrons with opposite spins. s holds 2, p holds 6, d holds 10."],
["Write the electron configurations of chromium and copper and say why they are unusual.","Cr: [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹.  Cu: [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹. A half-filled or filled 3d sub-shell is more stable than the expected 3d⁴4s² or 3d⁹4s²."],
["What is periodicity?","A repeating pattern of physical and chemical properties across each period of the periodic table."]],
"WCH11/bonding-and-structure":[
["Define ionic bonding.","The strong electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions."],
["What affects the strength of ionic bonding?","Ionic charge and ionic radius. Smaller, more highly charged ions attract more strongly."],
["Define a covalent bond and a dative covalent bond.","Covalent: the electrostatic attraction between two nuclei and a shared pair of electrons. Dative: a covalent bond in which both shared electrons come from the same atom."],
["Define metallic bonding.","The strong electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons."],
["State the principle of electron-pair repulsion theory.","Electron pairs around a central atom repel each other and get as far apart as possible. Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs."],
["Give the shape and bond angle of CH₄, NH₃ and H₂O.","CH₄: tetrahedral, 109.5°. NH₃: trigonal pyramidal, 107°. H₂O: bent (V-shaped), 104.5°."],
["Define electronegativity.","The ability of an atom to attract the bonding electrons in a covalent bond."],
["Why can a molecule have polar bonds but not be polar overall?","If the molecule is symmetrical, the dipoles cancel, so there is no overall dipole (e.g. CO₂, CCl₄)."],
["Why do giant covalent structures such as diamond have very high melting temperatures?","Many strong covalent bonds must be broken, which needs a lot of energy."],
["Why does graphite conduct electricity but diamond does not?","In graphite each carbon forms three bonds, leaving one delocalised electron per atom that can move between layers. In diamond all four outer electrons are in covalent bonds."]],
"WCH11/introductory-organic-chemistry-and-alkanes":[
["What is a homologous series?","A family of compounds with the same functional group and general formula, where each member differs from the next by CH₂ and physical properties show a trend."],
["What is structural isomerism?","Compounds with the same molecular formula but different structural formulae."],
["What is the general formula of the alkanes, and why are they described as saturated?","CₙH₂ₙ₊₂. They contain only single bonds between carbon atoms."],
["Describe homolytic and heterolytic bond fission.","Homolytic: the bond breaks evenly and each atom takes one electron, forming two radicals. Heterolytic: one atom takes both electrons, forming a positive and a negative ion."],
["What is a radical?","A species with an unpaired electron."],
["Name the three stages of the free-radical substitution of methane with chlorine and write the initiation step.","Initiation, propagation, termination. Initiation: Cl₂ → 2Cl• (needs UV light)."],
["Write the two propagation steps for methane and chlorine.","Cl• + CH₄ → •CH₃ + HCl, then •CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•."],
["Why is free-radical substitution a poor way to make a single pure product?","Further substitution happens and termination steps give different products, so a mixture forms."],
["What are cracking and reforming?","Cracking: breaking long-chain alkanes into shorter alkanes and alkenes. Reforming: converting straight-chain alkanes into branched and cyclic hydrocarbons that burn more efficiently."],
["Name two pollutants formed when alkane fuels burn and a problem each causes.","Any two of: carbon monoxide (toxic); oxides of nitrogen and sulfur dioxide (acid rain); carbon particulates (breathing problems); unburned hydrocarbons (smog)."]],
"WCH11/alkenes":[
["What is the general formula of the alkenes, and why are they unsaturated?","CₙH₂ₙ. They contain a carbon–carbon double bond."],
["Describe the two parts of a C=C double bond.","A σ bond from head-on overlap of orbitals, and a π bond from sideways overlap of p orbitals above and below the plane of the atoms."],
["Why do alkenes show geometric (E/Z) isomerism?","Rotation about the C=C bond is restricted, and each carbon of the double bond has two different groups attached."],
["What is an electrophile?","A species that accepts a pair of electrons to form a covalent bond; it is attracted to regions of high electron density."],
["What do you see when bromine water is shaken with an alkene, and what type of reaction is it?","The orange-brown colour disappears (decolourises). Electrophilic addition."],
["State the products of an alkene reacting with (a) hydrogen, (b) steam, (c) a hydrogen halide.","(a) An alkane (nickel catalyst). (b) An alcohol (acid catalyst). (c) A halogenoalkane."],
["When HBr adds to propene, which is the major product and why?","2-bromopropane. It forms through the secondary carbocation, which is more stable than the primary one because more alkyl groups push electrons towards the positive carbon."],
["What forms when an alkene reacts with acidified potassium manganate(VII)?","A diol. The purple solution turns colourless."],
["What is addition polymerisation?","Many alkene monomers join together as their double bonds open, forming a long chain with no other product."],
["Give two ways of dealing with waste polymers other than landfill.","Any two of: recycling; burning to release energy (incineration); using as a chemical feedstock (cracking)."]],
"WCH12/energetics":[
["Define enthalpy change and state the standard conditions.","The heat energy change at constant pressure. Standard conditions: 100 kPa, a stated temperature (usually 298 K), and substances in their standard states."],
["Define standard enthalpy change of formation.","The enthalpy change when one mole of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states, under standard conditions."],
["Define standard enthalpy change of combustion.","The enthalpy change when one mole of a substance is completely burned in oxygen under standard conditions."],
["Define standard enthalpy change of neutralisation.","The enthalpy change when an acid and an alkali react to form one mole of water under standard conditions."],
["State Hess’s law.","The enthalpy change for a reaction is independent of the route taken, provided the initial and final conditions are the same."],
["How do you calculate the energy transferred in a calorimetry experiment, and then ΔH?","q = mcΔT (m is the mass of the solution or water heated). ΔH = −q ÷ moles reacted, converted to kJ mol⁻¹."],
["Define bond enthalpy and mean bond enthalpy.","Bond enthalpy: the energy needed to break one mole of a bond in the gas phase. Mean bond enthalpy: the average for that bond over a range of compounds."],
["How do you estimate ΔH from bond enthalpies, and why is the answer approximate?","ΔH = sum of bonds broken − sum of bonds made. It is approximate because mean bond enthalpies are averages and the substances may not be gases."],
["Sketch in words the enthalpy level diagram for an exothermic reaction.","Reactants are higher than products. ΔH is negative and shown as a downward arrow; the activation energy is the hump from reactants to the peak."]],
"WCH12/intermolecular-forces":[
["How do London (dispersion) forces arise?","Random movement of electrons creates an instantaneous dipole in one molecule, which induces a dipole in a neighbouring molecule; the two attract."],
["What affects the strength of London forces?","The number of electrons, and the surface area of contact between molecules. More electrons and more contact give stronger forces."],
["What is needed for hydrogen bonding to occur?","A hydrogen atom bonded to N, O or F, and a lone pair on an N, O or F atom of another molecule."],
["Why is the boiling temperature of water much higher than that of H₂S?","Water forms hydrogen bonds between molecules, which are stronger than the London and dipole forces in H₂S, so more energy is needed to separate the molecules."],
["Why is ice less dense than liquid water?","In ice the hydrogen bonds hold the molecules in an open lattice, further apart than in the liquid."],
["Why do branched alkanes have lower boiling temperatures than their straight-chain isomers?","Branched molecules cannot pack as closely, so there is less surface contact and weaker London forces."],
["Why are alcohols less volatile than alkanes of similar molar mass, and why are short-chain alcohols soluble in water?","Alcohols form hydrogen bonds between molecules; alkanes have only London forces. Short-chain alcohols can hydrogen-bond with water."],
["Why are non-polar substances insoluble in water?","They cannot form hydrogen bonds with water, and the strong hydrogen bonds between water molecules would have to be broken."]],
"WCH12/redox-chemistry-groups-1-2-7":[
["Define oxidation and reduction in terms of electrons and of oxidation number.","Oxidation: loss of electrons; oxidation number increases. Reduction: gain of electrons; oxidation number decreases."],
["What is a disproportionation reaction? Give an example.","The same element is both oxidised and reduced in one reaction, e.g. Cl₂ + 2NaOH → NaCl + NaClO + H₂O."],
["Describe and explain the trend in reactivity down Group 2.","Reactivity increases. The outer electrons are further from the nucleus and more shielded, so ionisation energies decrease and electrons are lost more easily."],
["State the trends in solubility of Group 2 hydroxides and sulfates down the group.","Hydroxides become more soluble; sulfates become less soluble."],
["State and explain the trend in thermal stability of Group 2 carbonates and nitrates.","Stability increases down the group. Larger cations have a lower charge density, so they polarise (distort) the anion less."],
["Give the flame colours of lithium, sodium, potassium, calcium, strontium and barium.","Li red, Na yellow, K lilac, Ca brick-red (orange-red), Sr crimson red, Ba pale green."],
["State and explain the trend in boiling temperature down Group 7.","It increases. The molecules have more electrons, so London forces are stronger."],
["State and explain the trend in oxidising power of the halogens.","It decreases down the group. The atoms are larger with more shielding, so they attract an extra electron less easily."],
["What is seen when silver nitrate solution is added to chloride, bromide and iodide ions, and how does ammonia distinguish them?","Chloride: white precipitate, dissolves in dilute ammonia. Bromide: cream precipitate, dissolves in concentrated ammonia. Iodide: yellow precipitate, insoluble in ammonia."],
["How does the reducing power of the halide ions change down the group, as shown by their reaction with concentrated sulfuric acid?","It increases. Chloride does not reduce the acid; bromide reduces it to SO₂; iodide reduces it as far as S and H₂S."]],
"WCH12/introduction-to-kinetics-and-equilibria":[
["State what collision theory says is needed for a reaction.","Particles must collide with energy equal to or greater than the activation energy, and with the correct orientation."],
["Define activation energy.","The minimum energy that colliding particles need for a reaction to occur."],
["Use the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution to explain why a small rise in temperature greatly increases rate.","The curve flattens and shifts right, so a much larger proportion of molecules have energy at or above the activation energy. Collisions are also slightly more frequent."],
["How does a catalyst increase the rate of reaction?","It provides an alternative route with a lower activation energy, so more molecules have enough energy to react. It is not used up."],
["What does the area under a Maxwell–Boltzmann curve represent?","The total number of molecules. It stays the same when temperature changes."],
["What is meant by dynamic equilibrium?","In a closed system, the forward and reverse reactions occur at the same rate, so the concentrations of reactants and products stay constant."],
["Predict the effect of increasing pressure on an equilibrium involving gases.","The position of equilibrium moves towards the side with fewer moles of gas, which opposes the increase in pressure."],
["Predict the effect of increasing temperature on an equilibrium.","The position moves in the endothermic direction, which absorbs the added heat."],
["Why might an industrial process use a compromise temperature?","A low temperature may give a high yield but a slow rate; a higher temperature gives a faster rate with an acceptable yield. Cost and safety also matter."]],
"WCH12/organic-chemistry-halogenoalkanes-alcohols-and-spectra":[
["What is a nucleophile?","A species that donates a lone pair of electrons to form a covalent bond; it attacks electron-deficient atoms."],
["Why are halogenoalkanes attacked by nucleophiles?","The carbon–halogen bond is polar. The carbon atom is δ+ and attracts the nucleophile’s lone pair."],
["State the reagents and conditions for making an alcohol and for making an alkene from a halogenoalkane.","Alcohol: aqueous KOH or NaOH, heat under reflux (nucleophilic substitution). Alkene: KOH in ethanol, heat (elimination)."],
["How does the rate of hydrolysis of halogenoalkanes change from chloro- to iodo-, and why?","Iodoalkanes react fastest. The C–I bond is the weakest (lowest bond enthalpy), so it breaks most easily."],
["Classify alcohols as primary, secondary or tertiary.","By the number of carbon atoms attached to the carbon carrying the OH group: one (primary), two (secondary), three (tertiary)."],
["What are the products of oxidising primary, secondary and tertiary alcohols with acidified potassium dichromate(VI)?","Primary: aldehyde (distil) then carboxylic acid (reflux). Secondary: ketone. Tertiary: not oxidised. The colour change is orange to green."],
["Why is reflux used, and why is distillation used, when oxidising a primary alcohol?","Reflux keeps volatile substances in the flask so oxidation goes fully to the carboxylic acid. Distillation removes the aldehyde as it forms, before it can be oxidised further."],
["What does the molecular ion peak in a mass spectrum tell you?","The relative molecular mass of the compound (the peak with the highest m/z, ignoring small isotope peaks)."],
["How is infrared spectroscopy used to identify functional groups?","Bonds absorb infrared at characteristic wavenumbers. For example O–H in alcohols gives a broad peak around 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ and C=O gives a strong peak around 1700 cm⁻¹."],
["How can you make a chloroalkane from an alcohol?","React the alcohol with PCl₅, or a tertiary alcohol with concentrated hydrochloric acid."]],
"WCH14/kinetics":[
["Define rate of reaction and order of reaction.","Rate: the change in concentration of a reactant or product per unit time. Order with respect to a reactant: the power to which its concentration is raised in the rate equation."],
["Write a general rate equation and define the rate constant.","rate = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ. k is the rate constant, which is fixed at a given temperature; m and n are the orders."],
["What do zero, first and second order mean for how rate changes when concentration doubles?","Zero: rate unchanged. First: rate doubles. Second: rate quadruples."],
["How can you recognise a first-order reaction from a concentration–time graph?","The half-life is constant."],
["What is the rate-determining step?","The slowest step in a multi-step mechanism; it controls the overall rate."],
["How does the rate equation link to the mechanism?","Species in the rate equation are those involved in, or before, the rate-determining step, with orders equal to the number of each taking part."],
["Write the Arrhenius equation in its logarithmic form and say how it is used.","ln k = −Ea/RT + ln A. Plot ln k against 1/T: the gradient is −Ea/R, giving the activation energy."],
["What do the rate equations tell you about SN1 and SN2 hydrolysis of halogenoalkanes?","SN1 (tertiary): rate = k[halogenoalkane], one species in the slow step. SN2 (primary): rate = k[halogenoalkane][OH⁻], both species in the slow step."],
["Name two experimental methods for following a reaction rate.","Any two of: colorimetry; measuring gas volume or mass loss; titrating quenched samples; a clock reaction (initial rates)."]],
"WCH14/entropy-and-energetics":[
["What is entropy?","A measure of the disorder of a system: the number of ways the particles and their energy can be arranged."],
["How does entropy change from solid to liquid to gas, and when the number of gas moles increases?","Entropy increases from solid to liquid to gas, and increases when a reaction produces more moles of gas."],
["Write the equations for the entropy change of the surroundings and the total entropy change.","ΔS(surroundings) = −ΔH/T.  ΔS(total) = ΔS(system) + ΔS(surroundings)."],
["What is the condition for a reaction to be feasible, in terms of entropy and of Gibbs energy?","ΔS(total) must be positive, or ΔG must be negative (ΔG = ΔH − TΔS(system))."],
["Why might a reaction that is thermodynamically feasible not happen?","The activation energy may be too high, so the rate is too slow (kinetic stability)."],
["Define lattice energy.","The energy change when one mole of an ionic solid is formed from its gaseous ions."],
["Why may a theoretical lattice energy differ from the Born–Haber value?","The theoretical model assumes purely ionic bonding with spherical ions. A difference shows some covalent character, caused by polarisation of the anion."],
["What makes a cation strongly polarising and an anion easily polarised?","Cation: small radius and high charge. Anion: large radius and high charge."],
["Define enthalpy change of solution and enthalpy change of hydration.","Solution: the enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid dissolves in enough water that the ions are well separated. Hydration: the enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous ions dissolves in water to form an infinitely dilute solution."],
["How are lattice energy, hydration enthalpy and enthalpy of solution linked?","ΔH(solution) = sum of hydration enthalpies of the ions − lattice energy (of formation)."]],
"WCH14/chemical-equilibria":[
["Write the expression for Kc for the reaction aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD.","Kc = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / [A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ, using equilibrium concentrations."],
["What is partial pressure and how is it calculated?","The pressure a gas in a mixture would exert if it occupied the container alone. Partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure."],
["Write the expression for Kp for N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g).","Kp = p(NH₃)² / (p(N₂) × p(H₂)³)."],
["Which species are left out of Kc and Kp expressions for heterogeneous equilibria?","Pure solids and pure liquids, because their concentrations are constant. Kp includes gases only."],
["What is the only factor that changes the value of an equilibrium constant?","Temperature. Concentration, pressure and catalysts do not change K."],
["How does K change with temperature for an exothermic forward reaction?","K decreases as temperature increases (the equilibrium moves in the endothermic, reverse direction)."],
["What effect does a catalyst have on an equilibrium?","None on the position or on K. It speeds up the forward and reverse reactions equally, so equilibrium is reached faster."],
["How is the equilibrium constant linked to total entropy change?","ΔS(total) = R ln K. A large positive ΔS(total) means a large K, with products favoured."]],
"WCH14/acid-base-equilibria":[
["Define a Brønsted–Lowry acid and base.","An acid is a proton donor; a base is a proton acceptor."],
["What is a conjugate acid–base pair?","Two species that differ by one proton (H⁺), e.g. CH₃COOH and CH₃COO⁻."],
["Define pH and state how to find [H⁺] from pH.","pH = −log₁₀[H⁺].  [H⁺] = 10^(−pH)."],
["What is the difference between a strong and a weak acid?","A strong acid is fully dissociated in solution; a weak acid is only partially dissociated."],
["Write the expression for Ka of a weak acid HA, and define pKa.","Ka = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA].  pKa = −log₁₀Ka. A smaller pKa means a stronger acid."],
["Give the expression and value of Kw at 298 K.","Kw = [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ mol² dm⁻⁶."],
["How do you calculate the pH of a strong base such as 0.10 mol dm⁻³ NaOH?","[OH⁻] = 0.10, so [H⁺] = Kw/[OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹³, and pH = 13."],
["What is a buffer solution and what is it made from?","A solution that resists changes in pH when small amounts of acid or alkali are added. An acidic buffer is a weak acid and one of its salts (its conjugate base)."],
["Explain how an acidic buffer resists a fall in pH when acid is added.","The added H⁺ ions react with the large reservoir of A⁻ ions to form HA, so [H⁺] hardly changes."],
["How do you choose an indicator for a titration?","Its colour-change pH range must lie within the vertical section of the titration curve. Use methyl orange for strong acid–weak base and phenolphthalein for weak acid–strong base."]],
"WCH14/organic-chemistry-carbonyls-carboxylic-acids-and-chirality":[
["What is a chiral centre?","A carbon atom bonded to four different atoms or groups."],
["What are optical isomers, and how do they differ?","Non-superimposable mirror images of each other (enantiomers). They rotate the plane of plane-polarised light in opposite directions."],
["What is a racemic mixture and why is it optically inactive?","An equimolar mixture of two enantiomers. Their rotations are equal and opposite, so they cancel."],
["Why does an SN1 reaction of a chiral halogenoalkane give a racemic mixture?","It goes through a planar carbocation, which the nucleophile can attack equally from either side."],
["How do you distinguish an aldehyde from a ketone?","Warm with Tollens’ reagent (silver mirror with an aldehyde) or Fehling’s/Benedict’s solution (blue to red precipitate with an aldehyde). Ketones give no change."],
["What is the test for a carbonyl group, and what is seen?","Add 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (Brady’s reagent). An orange or yellow precipitate forms."],
["What reduces aldehydes and ketones, and to what?","Lithium tetrahydridoaluminate (LiAlH₄) in dry ether. Aldehydes give primary alcohols; ketones give secondary alcohols."],
["Describe the reaction of carbonyl compounds with HCN.","Nucleophilic addition, using HCN with KCN. A hydroxynitrile forms. With an unsymmetrical carbonyl the product is a racemic mixture because the planar C=O is attacked from both sides."],
["What does a positive iodoform test show?","Pale yellow crystals with iodine and alkali show a CH₃C=O group (methyl ketone or ethanal) or a CH₃CH(OH) group."],
["How are esters made from a carboxylic acid, and from an acyl chloride?","Acid + alcohol with a concentrated sulfuric acid catalyst, heated (reversible). Acyl chloride + alcohol at room temperature (goes to completion, HCl also forms)."],
["Give the products when an acyl chloride reacts with water, ammonia and an amine.","Water: carboxylic acid. Ammonia: amide. Amine: N-substituted amide. HCl is formed each time."]],
"WCH15/redox-equilibria":[
["Define standard electrode potential.","The e.m.f. of a half-cell measured against the standard hydrogen electrode under standard conditions: 298 K, 100 kPa, solutions of 1 mol dm⁻³."],
["Describe the standard hydrogen electrode.","Hydrogen gas at 100 kPa bubbled over a platinum electrode in a solution of 1 mol dm⁻³ H⁺ ions at 298 K. Its potential is defined as 0 V."],
["How do you calculate the e.m.f. of a cell from electrode potentials?","E(cell) = E(more positive half-cell, where reduction occurs) − E(more negative half-cell, where oxidation occurs)."],
["How do you use E values to decide whether a reaction is feasible?","The reaction is feasible if E(cell) is positive: the species in the more positive half-cell is reduced."],
["Why might a reaction with a positive E(cell) not take place?","The rate may be too slow (high activation energy), or the conditions are not standard."],
["How is E(cell) related to total entropy change and to the equilibrium constant?","E(cell) is directly proportional to ΔS(total) and to ln K."],
["What is the job of a salt bridge?","It completes the circuit by letting ions move between the half-cells, without the solutions mixing."],
["What is a fuel cell? Give the overall reaction in a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell.","A cell that produces a voltage from the reaction of a fuel with oxygen, with the reactants supplied continuously. 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O."],
["Why is acid added and no indicator needed in a manganate(VII) titration?","H⁺ ions are needed for MnO₄⁻ to be reduced to Mn²⁺. The manganate(VII) is its own indicator: the first permanent pale pink shows the end point."],
["How are iodine–thiosulfate titrations used?","An oxidising agent liberates iodine from excess iodide; the iodine is titrated with thiosulfate (2S₂O₃²⁻ + I₂ → S₄O₆²⁻ + 2I⁻), adding starch near the end point (blue-black to colourless)."]],
"WCH15/transition-metals-and-their-chemistry":[
["Define a transition metal.","A d-block element that forms at least one stable ion with an incompletely filled d sub-shell."],
["Why are scandium and zinc not classed as transition metals?","Sc³⁺ has an empty d sub-shell (3d⁰) and Zn²⁺ has a full one (3d¹⁰)."],
["Give four characteristic properties of transition metals.","Variable oxidation states, coloured ions, formation of complex ions, and catalytic activity."],
["Define a ligand and a complex ion.","Ligand: a molecule or ion that donates a lone pair to form a dative bond with a central metal ion. Complex ion: a central metal ion surrounded by ligands."],
["What is coordination number? Give the usual shapes for 6 and 4.","The number of dative bonds to the central metal ion. Six: octahedral. Four: tetrahedral (e.g. with Cl⁻) or square planar (e.g. cisplatin)."],
["Why are transition metal complexes coloured?","Ligands split the d orbitals into two energy levels. Electrons absorb visible light to move to the higher level, and the complementary colour is seen."],
["Name three things that change the colour of a complex.","The oxidation state of the metal, the ligand, and the coordination number."],
["What are monodentate, bidentate and multidentate ligands? Give an example of each.","They form one, two or many dative bonds. Examples: H₂O or NH₃; 1,2-diaminoethane; EDTA⁴⁻."],
["Why does replacing monodentate ligands with a multidentate ligand give a more stable complex?","The number of particles in solution increases, so the entropy of the system increases (the chelate effect)."],
["What is seen when aqueous sodium hydroxide is added to Cu²⁺, Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ ions?","Cu²⁺: blue precipitate. Fe²⁺: green precipitate (turns brown on standing). Fe³⁺: brown precipitate."],
["What is the difference between a heterogeneous and a homogeneous catalyst?","Heterogeneous: in a different phase from the reactants (reactants adsorb on its surface). Homogeneous: in the same phase, and works through an intermediate by changing oxidation state."]],
"WCH15/organic-chemistry-arenes":[
["Describe the bonding in benzene.","Each carbon forms three σ bonds. The remaining p orbitals overlap sideways to form a delocalised ring of π electrons above and below the plane. All carbon–carbon bonds are the same length."],
["Give two pieces of evidence against the Kekulé structure of benzene.","Any two of: all C–C bonds are the same length; the enthalpy of hydrogenation is less exothermic than expected for three double bonds; benzene does not readily decolourise bromine water."],
["Why does benzene undergo substitution rather than addition?","Addition would destroy the stable delocalised π system; substitution keeps it."],
["State the reagents for the nitration of benzene and the electrophile formed.","Concentrated nitric acid and concentrated sulfuric acid, about 50 °C. The electrophile is the nitronium ion, NO₂⁺."],
["What is needed to brominate benzene, and why?","Bromine with a halogen carrier such as FeBr₃ or iron. The carrier polarises the bromine to generate the Br⁺ electrophile, because benzene’s delocalised electrons are not electron-dense enough to polarise Br₂ alone."],
["What are Friedel–Crafts reactions?","Alkylation (with a halogenoalkane) or acylation (with an acyl chloride) of benzene, using an AlCl₃ catalyst in anhydrous conditions. They form a new C–C bond to the ring."],
["Why does phenol react with bromine water without a catalyst?","A lone pair on the oxygen is delocalised into the ring, increasing its electron density, so the ring is more easily attacked by electrophiles."],
["What is seen when bromine water is added to phenol?","The bromine water is decolourised and a white precipitate (2,4,6-tribromophenol) forms."]],
"WCH15/organic-nitrogen-compounds":[
["Why are amines bases?","The lone pair on the nitrogen atom can accept a proton."],
["Put ammonia, a primary aliphatic amine and phenylamine in order of base strength and explain.","Phenylamine < ammonia < primary aliphatic amine. Alkyl groups push electrons towards N, making the lone pair more available. In phenylamine the lone pair is delocalised into the ring, making it less available."],
["Give two ways of making a primary aliphatic amine.","React a halogenoalkane with excess ammonia in ethanol, or reduce a nitrile (LiAlH₄ in dry ether, or hydrogen with a catalyst)."],
["How is phenylamine made from nitrobenzene?","Reduce with tin and concentrated hydrochloric acid, heated under reflux, then add sodium hydroxide."],
["How are amides formed?","React an acyl chloride with ammonia (primary amide) or with an amine (N-substituted amide)."],
["What is a zwitterion?","An ion with both a positive and a negative charge but no overall charge. Amino acids form them: the COOH loses H⁺ and the NH₂ gains it."],
["How do amino acids behave in acidic and in alkaline solution?","In acid the amine group is protonated (positive ion). In alkali the carboxylic acid group loses a proton (negative ion)."],
["What is a peptide bond and how is it broken?","The –CONH– link formed by condensation between the COOH of one amino acid and the NH₂ of another. It is broken by hydrolysis, e.g. heating with concentrated hydrochloric acid."],
["What are condensation polymers? Name two types.","Polymers formed when monomers join with the loss of a small molecule such as water or HCl. Polyesters and polyamides."]],
"WCH15/organic-synthesis":[
["Why are Grignard reagents and nitriles useful in synthesis?","They let you extend the carbon chain by forming new carbon–carbon bonds."],
["How is a Grignard reagent made, and what does it give with CO₂, an aldehyde and a ketone?","React a halogenoalkane with magnesium in dry ether. With CO₂: a carboxylic acid. With an aldehyde: a secondary alcohol (methanal gives primary). With a ketone: a tertiary alcohol. Each is followed by dilute acid."],
["What is heating under reflux and why is it used?","Heating a mixture with a vertical condenser so vapour condenses and returns to the flask. It allows long heating without losing volatile reactants or products."],
["How is an impure organic liquid usually purified?","Wash in a separating funnel, dry with an anhydrous salt such as magnesium sulfate, then distil and collect the fraction at the correct boiling temperature."],
["Outline recrystallisation.","Dissolve the solid in the minimum volume of hot solvent, filter hot if needed, cool to crystallise, filter under reduced pressure, wash with a little cold solvent and dry."],
["How can you check the purity of a solid product?","Measure its melting temperature. A pure solid melts sharply at the data-book value; impurities lower and broaden the range."],
["What does a high-resolution proton NMR spectrum tell you?","The number of peaks gives the number of hydrogen environments, the chemical shift the type of environment, the peak areas the ratio of hydrogens, and the splitting (n + 1 rule) the number of hydrogens on adjacent carbons."],
["What does a ¹³C NMR spectrum tell you?","The number of peaks equals the number of different carbon environments; chemical shift indicates the type of carbon."],
["How does chromatography separate substances, and what is an Rf value?","Components distribute differently between a mobile and a stationary phase, so they move at different speeds. Rf = distance moved by the spot ÷ distance moved by the solvent front."]],
"WBI11/biomolecules":[
["Why is water a good solvent and transport medium?","Water molecules are polar (dipoles), so they surround and dissolve ions and other polar molecules, which can then be carried in solution."],
["What are monosaccharides, disaccharides and polysaccharides? Give an example of each.","Single sugar units (glucose), two units joined by a glycosidic bond (maltose, sucrose, lactose), and many units joined in a long chain (starch, glycogen)."],
["How is a glycosidic bond formed and broken?","Formed by a condensation reaction, releasing water. Broken by hydrolysis, using water."],
["Name the two components of starch and describe their structure.","Amylose: unbranched chain of α-glucose with 1,4 glycosidic bonds, coiled into a helix. Amylopectin: branched, with 1,4 and 1,6 glycosidic bonds."],
["Why are starch and glycogen good storage molecules?","They are compact and insoluble, so they have no osmotic effect. Branching gives many ends for rapid hydrolysis to release glucose."],
["What is a triglyceride made of, and what bond joins the parts?","One glycerol and three fatty acids, joined by ester bonds formed in condensation reactions."],
["What is the difference between saturated and unsaturated fatty acids?","Saturated: no carbon–carbon double bonds. Unsaturated: one or more C=C double bonds, which put kinks in the chain."],
["How do high LDL and HDL levels relate to cardiovascular disease risk?","High LDL raises blood cholesterol and the risk of atheroma. HDL carries cholesterol to the liver for removal, so a higher HDL level is linked to lower risk."]],
"WBI11/circulation":[
["Why do large multicellular organisms need a mass transport system?","Their surface area to volume ratio is small and diffusion distances are too long, so diffusion alone is too slow to supply cells."],
["Compare the structure of arteries and veins.","Arteries: thick walls with much muscle and elastic tissue, narrow lumen, no valves. Veins: thin walls, wide lumen, valves to prevent backflow."],
["How are capillaries adapted for exchange?","Walls one cell thick give a short diffusion distance; they are very numerous and narrow, giving a large surface area and slow blood flow."],
["Name the three stages of the cardiac cycle.","Atrial systole, ventricular systole, and diastole."],
["What makes the atrioventricular valves close?","Ventricular pressure rising above atrial pressure when the ventricles contract."],
["Outline how an atheroma develops.","The artery endothelium is damaged; an inflammatory response brings white blood cells; cholesterol builds up to form an atheroma; calcium salts and fibres harden it into a plaque, which narrows the artery and raises blood pressure."],
["Outline the blood clotting process.","Platelets stick to the damaged wall and release thromboplastin. With calcium ions this converts prothrombin to thrombin. Thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen to insoluble fibrin, which forms a mesh that traps blood cells."],
["Why is the wall of the left ventricle thicker than the right?","It must generate a higher pressure to pump blood around the whole body, not just to the lungs."]],
"WBI11/health":[
["Name four risk factors for cardiovascular disease.","Any four of: high blood pressure, smoking, obesity, inactivity, a diet high in saturated fat or salt, genetics, age, sex."],
["What is the difference between correlation and causation?","Correlation: two variables change together. Causation: a change in one variable directly brings about the change in the other. Correlation alone does not prove causation."],
["How do you calculate body mass index?","BMI = mass in kg ÷ (height in m)²."],
["How do antihypertensives reduce risk, and give one drawback.","They lower blood pressure (e.g. ACE inhibitors, beta blockers, diuretics). Side effects can include dizziness, fainting or a cough."],
["How do statins reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease?","They inhibit an enzyme in the liver needed to make cholesterol, lowering blood LDL cholesterol."],
["How do anticoagulants and platelet inhibitors help, and what is the main risk?","They reduce blood clot formation (e.g. warfarin, aspirin). The risk is excessive bleeding."],
["What makes a health study valid and reliable?","A large, representative sample; control of other variables; a suitable control group; and results that are repeatable."],
["Why might people underestimate or overestimate a health risk?","Perception is affected by familiarity, whether the risk is voluntary, how far in the future the harm is, and media coverage, rather than by the actual probability."]],
"WBI11/gas-exchange-and-membrane-transport":[
["State Fick’s law in words.","Rate of diffusion is proportional to (surface area × concentration difference) ÷ thickness of the exchange surface."],
["How are the alveoli adapted for gas exchange?","Huge surface area; walls one cell thick; a dense capillary network and ventilation keep a steep concentration gradient."],
["Describe the fluid mosaic model of the cell membrane.","A phospholipid bilayer in which the molecules move freely (fluid), with proteins, cholesterol, glycoproteins and glycolipids scattered through it (mosaic)."],
["Define osmosis.","The net movement of free water molecules across a partially permeable membrane from a region of higher water potential (less solute) to lower water potential."],
["What is facilitated diffusion?","Passive movement of molecules down a concentration gradient through channel or carrier proteins. No ATP is needed."],
["How does active transport differ from diffusion?","It moves substances against their concentration gradient using carrier proteins and energy from ATP."],
["What are endocytosis and exocytosis?","Bulk transport using vesicles. Endocytosis takes material into the cell; exocytosis releases it when a vesicle fuses with the cell surface membrane. Both need ATP."],
["Why do phospholipids form a bilayer?","The phosphate heads are hydrophilic and face the water on each side; the fatty acid tails are hydrophobic and face inwards, away from water."]],
"WBI11/proteins-and-enzymes":[
["Draw in words the general structure of an amino acid.","A central carbon bonded to an amine group (NH₂), a carboxyl group (COOH), a hydrogen atom and a variable R group."],
["What is the primary structure of a protein, and what bond holds it together?","The sequence of amino acids in the polypeptide chain, joined by peptide bonds."],
["What are secondary and tertiary structure?","Secondary: folding into α-helices or β-pleated sheets, held by hydrogen bonds. Tertiary: further folding into a specific 3D shape, held by hydrogen, ionic and disulfide bonds and hydrophobic interactions between R groups."],
["Compare globular and fibrous proteins with examples.","Globular: compact, soluble, metabolically active (e.g. haemoglobin, enzymes). Fibrous: long chains, insoluble, strong and structural (e.g. collagen)."],
["How do enzymes speed up reactions?","They lower the activation energy by forming an enzyme–substrate complex at the active site."],
["Why are enzymes specific?","The active site has a specific shape that is complementary to only one substrate (or type of substrate)."],
["Explain the effect of increasing substrate concentration on the rate of an enzyme reaction.","Rate rises as more enzyme–substrate complexes form, then levels off when all active sites are occupied and enzyme concentration becomes limiting."],
["What happens to an enzyme at high temperature?","Bonds holding the tertiary structure break, the active site changes shape, and the substrate no longer fits: the enzyme is denatured."]],
"WBI11/nucleotides-and-nucleic-acids":[
["What are the three parts of a DNA nucleotide?","A deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group and a nitrogen-containing base (A, T, C or G)."],
["State the base-pairing rules in DNA and the bonds involved.","Adenine pairs with thymine (two hydrogen bonds); cytosine pairs with guanine (three hydrogen bonds)."],
["Give three differences between DNA and RNA.","DNA has deoxyribose, thymine and is double-stranded; RNA has ribose, uracil and is usually single-stranded."],
["What is meant by semi-conservative replication?","Each new DNA molecule contains one original strand and one newly made strand."],
["What do DNA helicase and DNA polymerase do?","Helicase breaks the hydrogen bonds between bases to unzip the strands. DNA polymerase joins the new nucleotides together by forming phosphodiester bonds."],
["How did Meselson and Stahl’s experiment support semi-conservative replication?","Bacteria grown in heavy nitrogen and moved to light nitrogen produced DNA of intermediate density after one generation, then intermediate and light bands after two."],
["What is a gene?","A sequence of bases on a DNA molecule that codes for the sequence of amino acids in a polypeptide."],
["What is a mutation, and how can it affect a protein?","A change in the base sequence of DNA. It may change an amino acid and so the protein’s shape and function, as in cystic fibrosis."]],
"WBI11/gene-expression":[
["Describe three features of the genetic code.","Triplet (three bases code for one amino acid), non-overlapping (each base is read once), and degenerate (most amino acids have more than one codon)."],
["Outline transcription.","The DNA unwinds; RNA nucleotides pair with the template (antisense) strand; RNA polymerase joins them to form mRNA, which leaves the nucleus through a nuclear pore."],
["Outline translation.","mRNA attaches to a ribosome. tRNA molecules with complementary anticodons bring specific amino acids, which are joined by peptide bonds until a stop codon is reached."],
["What is the role of tRNA?","It carries a specific amino acid to the ribosome and its anticodon pairs with the complementary codon on mRNA."],
["Define genotype, phenotype and allele.","Genotype: the alleles an organism has. Phenotype: its observable characteristics. Allele: an alternative form of a gene."],
["What do homozygous, heterozygous, dominant and recessive mean?","Homozygous: two identical alleles. Heterozygous: two different alleles. Dominant: expressed even with one copy. Recessive: expressed only when no dominant allele is present."],
["How does the cystic fibrosis mutation cause thick, sticky mucus?","The faulty CFTR protein cannot transport chloride ions out of cells, so water does not move out by osmosis and the mucus is not diluted."],
["Name two methods of prenatal genetic screening and one ethical issue.","Amniocentesis and chorionic villus sampling. Issues include the small risk of miscarriage and decisions about abortion."]],
"WBI12/cell-structure-and-microscopy":[
["Give three differences between prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells.","Prokaryotes have no nucleus (circular DNA free in the cytoplasm), no membrane-bound organelles and smaller (70S) ribosomes; many have plasmids and a cell wall."],
["State the function of the rough endoplasmic reticulum and the Golgi apparatus.","Rough ER: ribosomes on its surface make proteins, which it folds and transports. Golgi: modifies proteins and packages them into vesicles."],
["Describe the route of a protein that is secreted from a cell.","Made at ribosomes on the rough ER, carried in vesicles to the Golgi, modified, packaged into secretory vesicles, and released by exocytosis."],
["What is the function of mitochondria and of lysosomes?","Mitochondria: site of aerobic respiration, producing ATP. Lysosomes: contain digestive enzymes that break down unwanted material."],
["What is the difference between magnification and resolution?","Magnification is how many times bigger the image is than the object. Resolution is the ability to tell two close points apart."],
["Write the equation for magnification.","Magnification = image size ÷ actual size."],
["Why do electron microscopes show more detail than light microscopes?","Electrons have a much shorter wavelength than light, giving a much higher resolution."],
["How are cells organised in a multicellular organism?","Similar cells form tissues; tissues form organs; organs work together in organ systems."]],
"WBI12/reproduction-and-cell-division":[
["Why is mitosis important?","It produces genetically identical cells for growth, repair and asexual reproduction."],
["Name the stages of mitosis in order and say what happens in metaphase and anaphase.","Prophase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase. Metaphase: chromosomes line up on the equator. Anaphase: centromeres divide and chromatids are pulled to opposite poles."],
["What happens during interphase?","The cell grows, makes new organelles and proteins, and replicates its DNA."],
["Give two ways meiosis produces genetic variation.","Crossing over between homologous chromosomes, and independent assortment of chromosomes."],
["How are sperm cells specialised?","A flagellum for swimming, many mitochondria for energy, and an acrosome containing enzymes to digest the zona pellucida; the nucleus is haploid."],
["What are the acrosome reaction and the cortical reaction?","Acrosome reaction: enzymes are released to digest a path through the zona pellucida. Cortical reaction: after one sperm enters, cortical granules make the zona pellucida harden, preventing other sperm entering."],
["What does haploid mean, and why must gametes be haploid?","Having one set of chromosomes. At fertilisation two haploid gametes fuse to restore the diploid number."],
["Describe double fertilisation in flowering plants.","The pollen tube delivers two male nuclei. One fuses with the egg to form the zygote; the other fuses with the two polar nuclei to form the triploid endosperm."]],
"WBI12/development-and-inheritance":[
["What is a stem cell?","An undifferentiated cell that can keep dividing and can differentiate into specialised cells."],
["Distinguish totipotent, pluripotent and multipotent stem cells.","Totipotent: can form every cell type, including placental cells. Pluripotent: can form almost any body cell type but not a whole organism. Multipotent: can form a limited range of cell types."],
["How do cells become specialised?","Different genes are switched on. Active genes are transcribed to mRNA, which is translated into proteins that change the cell’s structure and function."],
["What is epigenetic modification? Give two examples.","A change in gene expression without a change in the DNA base sequence, e.g. DNA methylation and histone modification."],
["What is polygenic inheritance and what kind of variation does it produce?","A characteristic controlled by several genes at different loci. It gives continuous variation."],
["Give an example of how genotype and environment interact to affect phenotype.","Examples: human height depends on genes and diet; fur colour in some animals depends on temperature; cancers arise from genes and environmental factors."],
["What are sex-linked characteristics?","Characteristics controlled by genes on the sex chromosomes, usually the X. They appear more often in males, who have only one X chromosome."],
["Give one argument for and one against using embryonic stem cells.","For: they could treat currently incurable conditions. Against: embryos are destroyed, which some see as destroying a potential life."]],
"WBI12/the-structure-and-function-of-plants":[
["Name three structures found in plant cells but not in animal cells.","Cellulose cell wall, chloroplasts, large permanent vacuole (also amyloplasts, plasmodesmata, middle lamella)."],
["Describe the structure of cellulose.","Long unbranched chains of β-glucose joined by 1,4 glycosidic bonds. Hydrogen bonds between chains form strong microfibrils."],
["Compare the structure and function of xylem vessels and phloem sieve tubes.","Xylem: dead, hollow, lignified vessels carrying water and mineral ions upwards and giving support. Phloem: living sieve tube elements with companion cells, carrying sugars and other organic solutes up and down."],
["What is the function of sclerenchyma fibres?","Support. They are dead cells with thick lignified walls."],
["Why do plants need nitrate, magnesium and calcium ions?","Nitrate: to make amino acids, proteins and DNA. Magnesium: to make chlorophyll. Calcium: to make calcium pectate in the middle lamella."],
["What makes plant fibres useful, and why are they more sustainable than oil-based products?","They are strong and flexible. Plants are renewable and their products are biodegradable."],
["Describe William Withering’s contribution and how modern drug testing differs.","He tested foxglove (digitalis) on patients by trial and error to find a safe dose. Modern testing uses pre-clinical tests then three phases of clinical trials with placebos and double-blind design."],
["Why are a placebo and a double-blind trial used?","A placebo shows whether the drug works better than no treatment. Double-blind means neither patients nor doctors know who has the drug, removing bias."]],
"WBI12/biodiversity":[
["Define species.","A group of organisms with similar characteristics that can interbreed to produce fertile offspring."],
["What is biodiversity, and what is species richness?","The variety of living organisms in an area. Species richness is the number of different species in a habitat."],
["What is the difference between a habitat and a niche?","Habitat: the place where an organism lives. Niche: the role of a species in its habitat, including how it uses resources and interacts."],
["Name three types of adaptation with an example of each.","Anatomical (thick fur), behavioural (migration), physiological (producing antifreeze proteins or venom)."],
["Outline natural selection.","A population shows genetic variation due to mutation. A selection pressure means individuals with advantageous alleles are more likely to survive, reproduce and pass those alleles on. Their frequency increases over generations."],
["What does the Hardy–Weinberg equation let you calculate?","Allele and genotype frequencies in a population: p + q = 1 and p² + 2pq + q² = 1."],
["What are the three domains, and what evidence supports this classification?","Bacteria, Archaea and Eukaryota. It is based on molecular evidence, such as differences in ribosomal RNA and membrane structure."],
["How can genetic diversity within a species be measured?","By the number of different alleles or by the heterozygosity index (the proportion of heterozygotes in the population)."]],
"WBI12/populations-and-conservation":[
["What is the difference between in-situ and ex-situ conservation?","In-situ: conserving species in their natural habitat. Ex-situ: conserving them outside it, e.g. zoos and seed banks."],
["How do zoos help conserve endangered species?","Captive breeding programmes, reintroduction into the wild, research and education."],
["Why are studbooks used in captive breeding?","To choose mates that are not closely related, which avoids inbreeding and keeps genetic diversity."],
["How do seed banks conserve plants, and why are seeds tested regularly?","Seeds are dried and stored cold so they stay viable for a long time. Samples are germinated regularly to check they are still viable."],
["Give two advantages of storing seeds rather than whole plants.","Any two of: less space needed; cheaper; many individuals stored so more genetic diversity; less vulnerable to disease or vandalism."],
["What is meant by endemism?","A species that is found in only one geographical area."],
["Why does a small population face a higher risk of extinction?","The gene pool is small, so there is low genetic diversity and inbreeding, and the population is less able to adapt to change."],
["What is sustainability in the use of biological resources?","Using resources in a way that meets present needs without reducing their availability for future generations."]],
"WBI14/Photosynthesis":[
["Where do the light-dependent and light-independent reactions take place?","Light-dependent: thylakoid membranes. Light-independent (Calvin cycle): stroma."],
["What are the products of the light-dependent reactions?","ATP, reduced NADP and oxygen."],
["What is photolysis?","The splitting of water using light energy into hydrogen ions, electrons and oxygen."],
["Describe how ATP is made in the light-dependent reactions.","Excited electrons pass along the electron transport chain, releasing energy that pumps H⁺ into the thylakoid space. H⁺ ions flow back through ATP synthase, which makes ATP from ADP and Pi (chemiosmosis)."],
["Outline the Calvin cycle.","CO₂ combines with RuBP (catalysed by RUBISCO) to give two GP. GP is reduced to GALP using ATP and reduced NADP. Most GALP regenerates RuBP using ATP; some is used to make glucose and other compounds."],
["What is the difference between cyclic and non-cyclic photophosphorylation?","Cyclic uses only photosystem I and makes only ATP. Non-cyclic uses both photosystems and makes ATP, reduced NADP and oxygen."],
["What is a limiting factor? Name three for photosynthesis.","The factor in shortest supply that restricts the rate. Light intensity, carbon dioxide concentration and temperature."],
["How do an absorption spectrum and an action spectrum differ?","Absorption spectrum: how much light of each wavelength a pigment absorbs. Action spectrum: the rate of photosynthesis at each wavelength."]],
"WBI14/Ecosystems-and-biomass":[
["Define ecosystem, community and population.","Ecosystem: all the organisms and the non-living factors in an area. Community: all the populations of different species in a habitat. Population: all the organisms of one species in a habitat."],
["What is the difference between abiotic and biotic factors?","Abiotic: non-living factors such as light, temperature and pH. Biotic: living factors such as predation, competition and disease."],
["Define gross primary productivity and net primary productivity.","GPP: the rate at which producers convert light energy into chemical energy. NPP = GPP − energy lost in plant respiration; it is the energy available to the next trophic level."],
["Give three reasons why energy transfer between trophic levels is inefficient.","Energy is lost as heat from respiration; some parts are not eaten; some food is not digested and is lost in faeces."],
["How do you calculate the efficiency of energy transfer?","(Energy transferred to the next trophic level ÷ energy available at the previous level) × 100."],
["What is succession?","The change in the species making up a community over time, from pioneer species to a climax community."],
["How do pioneer species make succession possible?","They colonise bare ground, and when they die and decompose they form soil and add nutrients, making conditions suitable for other species."],
["What is the role of microorganisms in the carbon cycle?","Decomposers break down dead organic matter and release carbon dioxide through respiration."]],
"WBI14/Changes-to-populations-and-the-environment":[
["Name three greenhouse gases and explain the greenhouse effect.","Carbon dioxide, methane and water vapour. They absorb infrared radiation emitted by the Earth’s surface and re-emit it, warming the atmosphere."],
["Give two types of evidence for climate change.","Any two of: temperature records; carbon dioxide levels from ice cores; pollen in peat bogs; tree-ring widths (dendrochronology)."],
["How can rising temperature affect organisms?","It changes the rate of enzyme reactions and development, alters distribution of species, and changes the timing of life cycles."],
["Why are predictions from climate models uncertain?","They extrapolate from current data, rely on assumptions about future emissions, and many factors interact."],
["What is allopatric speciation?","A population is split by a geographical barrier; different selection pressures and mutations change allele frequencies until the groups can no longer interbreed."],
["What is sympatric speciation?","A new species forms without geographical separation, through reproductive isolation such as different breeding seasons or behaviour."],
["How can biofuels and reforestation help reduce climate change?","Biofuels are close to carbon neutral, as the CO₂ released was recently absorbed in photosynthesis. Reforestation removes CO₂ from the atmosphere."],
["Why does the scientific community use peer review?","Other scientists check the methods and conclusions before publication, so the findings are valid and reliable."]],
"WBI14/microbiology":[
["What is aseptic technique and why is it used?","Methods such as flaming the loop and working near a flame that prevent contamination of cultures and the release of microorganisms."],
["Name the four phases of a bacterial growth curve.","Lag, exponential (log), stationary and death (decline) phases."],
["Why does a bacterial population enter the stationary phase?","Nutrients run out and toxic waste builds up, so the death rate equals the division rate."],
["Give two ways of measuring the growth of a microbial culture.","Any two of: cell counts with a haemocytometer; dilution plating to count colonies; turbidity using a colorimeter; dry mass."],
["How do bacteria and viruses differ in structure?","Bacteria are cells with a cell wall, cytoplasm, ribosomes and circular DNA. Viruses are not cells: nucleic acid in a protein coat (capsid), sometimes with an envelope, and they replicate only inside host cells."],
["What is the difference between bactericidal and bacteriostatic antibiotics?","Bactericidal antibiotics kill bacteria. Bacteriostatic antibiotics stop them growing and reproducing."],
["How does antibiotic resistance develop in a bacterial population?","A random mutation gives resistance. When the antibiotic is used, resistant bacteria survive and reproduce, passing on the allele, so its frequency increases."],
["Give two ways hospitals reduce the spread of antibiotic-resistant infections.","Any two of: hand washing and hygiene codes; screening and isolating patients; prescribing antibiotics only when needed and completing the course."]],
"WBI14/disease-and-defence":[
["Name three non-specific defences against infection.","Any three of: skin, stomach acid, lysozyme in tears, inflammation, phagocytosis, interferon, fever."],
["Describe phagocytosis.","A phagocyte recognises and engulfs the pathogen into a vesicle (phagosome). Lysosomes fuse with it and enzymes digest the pathogen."],
["What is an antigen?","A molecule, usually a protein on a cell surface, that triggers an immune response."],
["What do T helper cells and T killer cells do?","T helper cells release cytokines that stimulate B cells and T killer cells to divide. T killer cells destroy infected body cells."],
["What do plasma cells and memory cells do?","Plasma cells secrete antibodies. Memory cells stay in the blood and give a faster, larger response if the same antigen is met again."],
["Compare active and passive immunity.","Active: the body makes its own antibodies and memory cells (infection or vaccination); long-lasting. Passive: antibodies are received from elsewhere (placenta, breast milk, injection); immediate but short-lived."],
["How does HIV weaken the immune system?","It infects and destroys T helper cells, so B cells and T killer cells are not activated effectively."],
["How does Mycobacterium tuberculosis evade the immune system?","It survives inside macrophages and can lie dormant within tubercles in the lungs, becoming active again if the immune system weakens."]],
"WBI14/forensic-techniques":[
["Name five things used to estimate time of death.","Body temperature, degree of muscle contraction (rigor mortis), extent of decomposition, forensic entomology and stage of succession."],
["How is body temperature used to estimate time of death, and what affects it?","The body cools after death in a predictable way. Cooling rate depends on body size, clothing, air temperature and position."],
["What causes rigor mortis?","After death, muscles run out of ATP, so the bonds between muscle proteins cannot be broken and the muscles stay contracted. It later passes off as the proteins break down."],
["How does forensic entomology help?","The species of insects present and the stage of their life cycle indicate how long the body has been dead."],
["Outline how a DNA profile is made.","DNA is extracted and amplified by PCR. Fragments are separated by size using gel electrophoresis and made visible, giving a pattern of bands."],
["Outline the polymerase chain reaction.","Heat to about 95 °C to separate the strands; cool to about 55 °C so primers bind; heat to about 70 °C so DNA polymerase builds new strands. The cycle is repeated many times."],
["How does gel electrophoresis separate DNA fragments?","DNA is negatively charged, so it moves towards the positive electrode. Smaller fragments move faster and further through the gel."],
["What are DNA profiles used for?","Identifying individuals in forensic cases, and showing genetic relationships between people or between species."]],
"WBI15/respiration":[
["Name the four stages of aerobic respiration and where each occurs.","Glycolysis (cytoplasm), link reaction and Krebs cycle (mitochondrial matrix), oxidative phosphorylation (inner mitochondrial membrane)."],
["What are the net products of glycolysis from one glucose molecule?","Two pyruvate, two ATP (net) and two reduced NAD."],
["What happens in the link reaction?","Pyruvate is decarboxylated and dehydrogenated to form acetyl groups, which join coenzyme A to make acetyl CoA. CO₂ and reduced NAD are produced."],
["What is produced in each turn of the Krebs cycle?","Two CO₂, three reduced NAD, one reduced FAD and one ATP."],
["Explain how ATP is made by chemiosmosis in oxidative phosphorylation.","Electrons from reduced NAD and FAD pass along the electron transport chain. Energy released pumps H⁺ into the intermembrane space. H⁺ flows back through ATP synthase, making ATP."],
["What is the role of oxygen in aerobic respiration?","It is the final electron acceptor; it combines with electrons and H⁺ to form water."],
["What happens to pyruvate in anaerobic respiration in animals, and why?","It is reduced to lactate, which regenerates NAD so glycolysis can continue."],
["Define respiratory quotient.","RQ = volume of CO₂ produced ÷ volume of O₂ used in the same time. About 1.0 for carbohydrates and 0.7 for lipids."]],
"WBI15/cardiac-and-skeletal-muscle":[
["What does myogenic mean?","The heart muscle contracts without needing a nerve impulse from outside."],
["Describe how the heartbeat is coordinated.","The SAN sends a wave of depolarisation across the atria, which contract. The AVN delays it, then passes it down the bundle of His and Purkyne fibres, so the ventricles contract from the apex upwards."],
["What do the P wave, QRS complex and T wave on an ECG represent?","P: depolarisation of the atria. QRS: depolarisation of the ventricles. T: repolarisation of the ventricles."],
["How do you calculate cardiac output?","Cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate."],
["Describe the sliding filament theory.","Calcium ions bind to troponin, moving tropomyosin and exposing binding sites on actin. Myosin heads bind, tilt to pull the actin along, then detach using ATP. The sarcomere shortens."],
["What is ATP needed for in muscle contraction?","To detach the myosin head from actin and return it to its original position, and to pump calcium ions back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum."],
["Compare fast twitch and slow twitch muscle fibres.","Slow twitch: many mitochondria and capillaries, much myoglobin, aerobic, fatigue slowly. Fast twitch: few mitochondria, little myoglobin, anaerobic, powerful but fatigue quickly."],
["What are antagonistic muscle pairs?","Two muscles that work against each other at a joint: when one contracts the other relaxes, e.g. biceps and triceps."]],
"WBI15/homeostatic-principles-and-thermoregulation":[
["Define homeostasis.","The maintenance of a stable internal environment within narrow limits, despite changes outside."],
["What is negative feedback?","A change from the normal level is detected and triggers a response that reverses the change, returning the level to normal."],
["What is positive feedback?","A change triggers a response that increases the change further, e.g. during blood clotting."],
["Which part of the brain controls body temperature, and how does it detect change?","The hypothalamus. It monitors blood temperature and receives impulses from temperature receptors in the skin."],
["Give three responses to an increase in body temperature.","Sweating increases, vasodilation of skin arterioles increases blood flow near the surface, and hairs lie flat."],
["Give three responses to a decrease in body temperature.","Shivering, vasoconstriction of skin arterioles, hairs raised, and a higher metabolic rate."],
["Why must core body temperature be kept constant?","Enzymes work fastest at their optimum temperature; too high and they denature, too low and reactions are too slow."],
["How is heart rate increased during exercise?","Chemoreceptors detect a fall in blood pH (more CO₂). The cardiovascular control centre in the medulla sends impulses along the sympathetic nerve to the SAN, which fires more often."]],
"WBI15/the-kidneys-osmoregulation-and-DNA-control":[
["What is ultrafiltration and where does it happen?","High pressure in the glomerulus forces small molecules such as water, glucose, urea and ions into the Bowman’s capsule. Blood cells and large proteins stay in the blood."],
["What is selective reabsorption, and where does most of it occur?","Useful substances are taken back into the blood. All the glucose and most water and ions are reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule."],
["What is the role of the loop of Henle?","It creates a high solute concentration in the medulla, so water can be reabsorbed from the collecting duct by osmosis."],
["How does ADH control the water content of the blood?","When blood water potential falls, osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus cause the pituitary to release more ADH. It makes the collecting duct walls more permeable to water, so more is reabsorbed and urine is more concentrated."],
["Why is urea produced?","Excess amino acids cannot be stored. They are deaminated in the liver, and the ammonia formed is converted to less toxic urea."],
["How do steroid hormones such as oestrogen affect cells?","They pass through the cell membrane, bind to a receptor inside the cell, and the complex acts as a transcription factor that switches genes on or off."],
["How do peptide hormones affect cells?","They bind to receptors on the cell surface membrane, activating a second messenger such as cyclic AMP inside the cell."],
["What is a transcription factor?","A protein that binds to DNA and controls whether a gene is transcribed."]],
"WBI15/structure-and-function-of-nerve-tissue-and-responses-to-stimuli":[
["What is the resting potential and how is it maintained?","About −70 mV across the axon membrane. The sodium–potassium pump moves 3 Na⁺ out for 2 K⁺ in, and the membrane is more permeable to K⁺, which diffuses out."],
["Describe depolarisation and repolarisation in an action potential.","Depolarisation: voltage-gated sodium channels open and Na⁺ diffuses in. Repolarisation: sodium channels close, potassium channels open and K⁺ diffuses out."],
["What is the refractory period and why does it matter?","A short time after an action potential when another cannot be generated. It keeps impulses separate and travelling in one direction."],
["How does myelination speed up conduction?","Depolarisation occurs only at the nodes of Ranvier, so the impulse jumps from node to node (saltatory conduction)."],
["Outline transmission across a synapse.","An impulse opens calcium channels; Ca²⁺ enters; vesicles release neurotransmitter by exocytosis; it diffuses across the cleft and binds to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, opening sodium channels."],
["How do rod cells respond to light?","Light breaks rhodopsin into retinal and opsin. Sodium channels close, the rod cell hyperpolarises and releases less inhibitory neurotransmitter, so the bipolar cell depolarises."],
["Compare nervous and hormonal coordination.","Nervous: electrical impulses, fast, short-lived, localised. Hormonal: chemicals in the blood, slower, longer-lasting, widespread."],
["What is the pupil reflex and why is it useful?","In bright light the circular muscles of the iris contract and the pupil narrows; in dim light the radial muscles contract and it widens. It protects the retina and controls the light reaching it."]],
"WBI15/plant-hormones-coordination-and-the-brain":[
["How does auxin (IAA) cause a shoot to bend towards light?","Auxin moves to the shaded side, where it makes cells elongate more, so the shoot bends towards the light."],
["What is phytochrome and how does it control flowering?","A pigment with two forms. Red light converts Pr to Pfr; far-red light or darkness converts it back. The amount of Pfr tells the plant the night length, which triggers or inhibits flowering."],
["State the main function of the cerebrum, cerebellum, medulla and hypothalamus.","Cerebrum: thought, memory, voluntary movement. Cerebellum: balance and coordination. Medulla: heart rate and breathing. Hypothalamus: temperature and water balance."],
["Compare CT, MRI and fMRI scans.","CT uses X-rays to show structure. MRI uses magnetic fields and radio waves for detailed images of soft tissue. fMRI shows brain activity by detecting changes in blood oxygen."],
["What is habituation?","A decreased response to a repeated harmless stimulus, as less neurotransmitter is released at the synapse."],
["What did Hubel and Wiesel’s work show?","There is a critical period in early life when visual stimulation is needed for the visual cortex to develop normally."],
["How does L-dopa treat Parkinson’s disease?","Parkinson’s involves a lack of dopamine. L-dopa crosses into the brain and is converted to dopamine."],
["How does MDMA (ecstasy) affect synapses?","It blocks the reuptake of serotonin, so serotonin stays in the synaptic cleft and keeps stimulating the postsynaptic neurone."]],
"WBI15/gene-technology":[
["What is recombinant DNA?","DNA made by joining DNA from two different sources."],
["What do restriction enzymes and DNA ligase do?","Restriction enzymes cut DNA at specific base sequences, often leaving sticky ends. Ligase joins DNA fragments together."],
["What is a vector in genetic modification? Give two examples.","Something that carries a gene into a cell, e.g. a plasmid or a virus."],
["How are bacteria that have taken up a recombinant plasmid identified?","A marker gene, such as antibiotic resistance or fluorescence, is included, so only transformed bacteria survive or glow."],
["What is a microarray used for?","To find which genes are active (expressed) in a cell, by seeing which labelled mRNA or cDNA binds to known DNA probes."],
["What is bioinformatics?","Using computers to store and analyse large amounts of biological data, such as DNA and protein sequences."],
["What are 'knockout' mice used for?","A gene is deactivated so its function can be studied from the effect on the animal."],
["Give one benefit and one risk of genetically modified crops.","Benefit: higher yield, better nutrition or pest resistance. Risk: transfer of genes to wild plants, reduced biodiversity, or unknown long-term effects."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-acceleration-of-a-freely-falling-object":[
["Describe a method to find g using a falling object and light gates (or an electromagnet and trapdoor).","Release the object from rest and measure the time t to fall a measured height h. Repeat for several heights and take repeats at each."],
["Which graph gives g, and how?","From h = ½gt², plot h against t² (or 2h against t²). The line is straight through the origin with gradient ½g (or g)."],
["Why is a small, dense ball used?","To make air resistance negligible compared with its weight."],
["Give one source of systematic error in the electromagnet method.","A delay in the ball being released as the magnetism decays, which makes the measured time too long and g too small."],
["How do you reduce the uncertainty in the measurements?","Use larger heights so times are longer, repeat and average, and time electronically rather than by hand."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-viscosity-of-a-liquid":[
["Outline the falling-ball method for measuring viscosity.","Drop a small sphere of known radius into a tall tube of the liquid and time it between two marks once it has reached terminal velocity. Repeat with spheres of different radius."],
["Which forces are balanced at terminal velocity, and what equation gives viscosity?","Weight = upthrust + Stokes drag. η = 2r²g(ρsphere − ρliquid)/9v."],
["Why must the first timing mark be well below the surface?","The ball must have reached terminal velocity before timing starts."],
["Why should the tube be wide and the ball small?","So the flow around the ball is laminar and the walls do not affect it; Stokes’ law then applies."],
["Why must the temperature be recorded and kept constant?","Viscosity depends strongly on temperature."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-youngs-modulus-of-a-material":[
["Outline how to measure the Young modulus of a wire.","Clamp a long thin wire, add known loads and measure the extension for each using a marker and scale (or vernier). Measure the original length and the diameter."],
["How is the diameter measured and why in several places?","With a micrometer, at several points and orientations, then averaged, because the wire may not be uniform. The diameter is squared in the area, so its uncertainty matters most."],
["How do you find the Young modulus from a graph?","Plot force against extension: E = gradient × L/A. Or plot stress against strain: E = gradient of the linear part."],
["Why is a long, thin wire used?","It gives a larger, more measurable extension for a given load, reducing percentage uncertainty."],
["Give one safety precaution.","Wear eye protection in case the wire snaps, and place padding under the masses."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-speed-of-sound-in-air":[
["Outline a method using two microphones and an oscilloscope.","Connect a signal generator to a loudspeaker and two microphones to a dual-beam oscilloscope. Move one microphone away until the traces are back in phase; the distance moved is one wavelength."],
["How is the speed then calculated?","Find the frequency from the signal generator or from the period on the oscilloscope (f = 1/T) and use v = fλ."],
["How can the uncertainty in the wavelength be reduced?","Move the microphone through several wavelengths and divide by the number counted."],
["How is the period read from an oscilloscope?","Count the horizontal divisions for one full cycle (or several cycles) and multiply by the time-base setting."],
["Name one factor that changes the speed of sound in air.","Air temperature."]],
"WPH13/standing-waves-on-a-vibrating-string":[
["Describe the apparatus used to investigate frequency and string properties.","A string fixed to a vibration generator (driven by a signal generator) passes over a pulley to hanging masses that provide tension. Adjust the frequency until a standing wave forms."],
["How is wavelength found from the standing wave?","The distance between adjacent nodes is half a wavelength. For the fundamental, λ = 2 × length."],
["State the equation for wave speed on a string.","v = √(T/μ), where T is the tension and μ the mass per unit length."],
["How does the fundamental frequency depend on length and tension?","f = (1/2L)√(T/μ): frequency is inversely proportional to length and proportional to the square root of tension."],
["How do you find the resonant frequency precisely?","Adjust the frequency slowly until the amplitude at the antinodes is a maximum and the nodes are sharp; approach from above and below."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-wavelength-of-light":[
["Outline how a diffraction grating is used to find the wavelength of laser light.","Shine the laser at right angles through the grating onto a distant screen. Measure the distance from the grating to the screen and from the central maximum to each order."],
["Which equation is used and how is the angle found?","nλ = d sin θ, with tan θ = (distance of order from centre) ÷ (grating-to-screen distance)."],
["How is the slit spacing d found?","d = 1 ÷ (number of lines per metre)."],
["How can the percentage uncertainty be reduced?","Use a large grating-to-screen distance, measure between the two same-order maxima on either side and halve it, and use higher orders."],
["State one laser safety precaution.","Never look into the beam or shine it at reflective surfaces; display a warning sign and keep the beam below eye level."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-electrical-resistivity-of-a-material":[
["Outline the method for finding the resistivity of a wire.","Measure the diameter with a micrometer. Measure the resistance (or V and I) for different lengths of wire set by a moving contact along a metre rule."],
["Which graph is plotted and how is resistivity obtained?","Resistance against length: a straight line through the origin. ρ = gradient × cross-sectional area."],
["Why is the current kept small and switched off between readings?","To stop the wire heating up, which would increase its resistance."],
["What causes a non-zero intercept on the graph?","Contact resistance or a zero error in the length measurement (systematic error)."],
["Which measurement contributes most to the uncertainty?","The diameter, because it is small and is squared when calculating area."]],
"WPH13/determining-the-emf-and-internal-resistance-of-an-electrical-cell":[
["Describe the circuit used.","The cell in series with an ammeter and a variable resistor, with a voltmeter across the cell’s terminals."],
["What readings are taken?","Terminal p.d. and current for a range of resistance settings."],
["Which graph is plotted and how are ε and r found?","V against I. From V = ε − Ir, the intercept on the V axis is the e.m.f. and the gradient is −r."],
["Why should the switch be opened between readings?","To avoid running the cell down or heating it, which would change its e.m.f. and internal resistance."],
["Why does the terminal p.d. fall as current increases?","More p.d. is dropped across the internal resistance ('lost volts' = Ir)."]],
"WPH16/investigating-change-in-momentum":[
["Outline a method to investigate force and rate of change of momentum.","A trolley on a friction-compensated runway is pulled by a falling mass over a pulley. Light gates measure its velocity at two points and the time between them."],
["What relationship is being tested?","Resultant force = change in momentum ÷ time taken (F = Δp/Δt)."],
["How is the total mass kept constant while the force is varied?","Masses are moved from the trolley to the hanger, so the accelerating force changes but the total mass does not."],
["Why is the runway tilted slightly?","To compensate for friction, so the trolley moves at constant velocity when given a push with no pulling force."],
["Which graph shows the relationship?","Force against rate of change of momentum (or FΔt against Δp): a straight line through the origin with gradient 1."]],
"WPH16/analysing-collisions-between-small-spheres":[
["How can a collision between two spheres be recorded for analysis?","Film it from above with a camera (video or strobe) against a grid or scale, then use frame-by-frame analysis to find positions and times."],
["How are the velocities found?","Distance moved between frames ÷ time between frames, with the direction measured from the images."],
["How is conservation of momentum tested in two dimensions?","Resolve each momentum into two perpendicular components and compare the totals before and after in each direction."],
["How can you tell whether the collision is elastic?","Compare total kinetic energy before and after; it is the same only for an elastic collision."],
["Give one source of error.","Parallax or lens distortion in the images, friction with the surface, or the spheres spinning."]],
"WPH16/capacitor-charging-and-discharging":[
["Outline how to investigate the discharge of a capacitor.","Charge the capacitor, then let it discharge through a resistor. Record the p.d. (or current) at regular times with a voltmeter and stopwatch or a data logger."],
["Which graph gives a straight line, and what is its gradient?","ln V against t. Gradient = −1/RC."],
["What is the time constant, and how can it be read from a V–t graph?","τ = RC: the time for V to fall to 37% (1/e) of its initial value."],
["Why is a data logger useful here?","It takes many readings at short, regular intervals, which is needed when the discharge is fast, and removes reaction-time error."],
["Why should the voltmeter have a very high resistance?","So that negligible charge flows through it; otherwise the capacitor discharges faster than through the resistor alone."]],
"WPH16/calibrating-a-thermistor-in-a-potential-divider-circuit":[
["Outline the calibration method.","Connect the thermistor in series with a fixed resistor across a supply. Place it in a water bath with a thermometer and record the output p.d. at a range of temperatures."],
["What is a calibration curve used for?","A graph of output p.d. against temperature, so that a p.d. reading can later be converted to a temperature."],
["How does the p.d. across an NTC thermistor change as temperature rises?","Its resistance falls, so it takes a smaller share of the supply p.d."],
["How do you make sure the thermistor is at the temperature shown by the thermometer?","Stir the water, keep the thermometer bulb next to the thermistor, and wait for readings to settle."],
["Why keep the current small?","To avoid the thermistor heating itself."]],
"WPH16/determining-the-specific-latent-heat-of-a-phase-change":[
["Outline a method for the specific latent heat of fusion of ice.","Heat ice in a funnel with an electrical heater of known power and collect the water melted in a measured time. Run an identical funnel without the heater on as a control."],
["Why is the control needed?","To find the mass melted by heat from the surroundings, which is subtracted from the mass melted with the heater on."],
["How is L calculated?","L = energy supplied ÷ mass melted by the heater = VIt ÷ Δm."],
["Why should the ice be at 0 °C and dry?","So no energy is used warming it, and so water already on the ice is not counted in the mass melted."],
["Give one reason the result may be too high.","Some energy from the heater is lost to the surroundings rather than melting ice."]],
"WPH16/investigating-the-pressure-and-volume-relationships-for-a-gas":[
["Outline the method.","Trap a fixed mass of gas in a sealed syringe or tube connected to a pressure gauge. Change the volume in steps and record the pressure each time."],
["What must be kept constant, and how?","Temperature and mass of gas. Change the volume slowly and wait before each reading so the gas returns to room temperature."],
["Which graph shows Boyle’s law?","p against 1/V is a straight line through the origin (pV = constant)."],
["How can the experiment be adapted to study pressure and temperature?","Keep the volume fixed, heat the gas in a water bath and record pressure at each temperature. Extrapolating the line to p = 0 gives absolute zero."],
["Give one source of error.","Gas leaking past the seal, or the volume of gas in the connecting tubing not being included."]],
"WPH16/investigating-the-absorption-of-gamma-radiation-by-lead":[
["Outline the method.","Place a gamma source a fixed distance from a Geiger–Müller tube. Put lead sheets of increasing total thickness between them and record the count for a fixed time."],
["What correction must be made to each reading?","Subtract the background count rate, measured with the source removed."],
["Which graph is plotted and what does the gradient give?","ln(corrected count rate) against thickness: a straight line with gradient −μ, the absorption coefficient."],
["Why are counts taken over a long time?","Decay is random, so a larger total count has a smaller percentage uncertainty."],
["State two safety precautions.","Handle the source with long tongs, point it away from people, keep it in a lead-lined box when not in use and minimise exposure time."]],
"WPH16/determining-the-value-of-an-unknown-mass-using-the-resonant-frequencies":[
["Outline the method.","Hang known masses on a spring and drive it with a vibration generator. For each mass, find the frequency at which the amplitude is greatest (resonance)."],
["Which equation links resonant frequency and mass?","f = (1/2π)√(k/m), so 1/f² is proportional to m."],
["How is the unknown mass found?","Plot 1/f² (or T²) against mass for the known masses, measure the resonant frequency with the unknown mass, and read its mass from the graph."],
["How do you locate the resonant frequency accurately?","Change the frequency in small steps near the peak and approach it from both sides."],
["Why does the graph not pass exactly through the origin?","The spring itself has mass, which also oscillates."]],
"WCH13/finding-the-molar-volume-gas":[
["Outline how to measure the molar volume of a gas.","React a known mass of a solid (e.g. calcium carbonate) with excess acid and collect the gas in a gas syringe or over water. Measure the final volume."],
["How is the molar volume calculated?","Molar volume = volume of gas collected ÷ moles of gas produced (from the moles of solid reacted)."],
["Why might the volume collected be too low?","Gas escapes before the bung is replaced, or some carbon dioxide dissolves in the water."],
["How can the loss of gas at the start be avoided?","Keep the reactants apart inside the sealed flask (e.g. the solid in a small tube) and tip to mix after the bung is in."],
["Which conditions must be recorded?","The temperature and pressure of the room, because gas volume depends on both."]],
"WCH13/enthalpy-change-reaction-hess-law":[
["Why is Hess’s law used to find some enthalpy changes?","Some reactions cannot be measured directly, so two other reactions that can be measured are combined in an energy cycle."],
["Outline the calorimetry measurement.","Add a weighed solid to a measured volume of excess acid in a polystyrene cup and record the temperature change. Repeat for the second substance."],
["How do you calculate the enthalpy change for each reaction?","q = mcΔT, then ΔH = −q ÷ moles of the limiting solid."],
["Why is a polystyrene cup with a lid used?","It is a good insulator, which reduces heat exchange with the surroundings."],
["How can a more accurate temperature change be found?","Record temperature at regular intervals before and after mixing and extrapolate the cooling line back to the time of mixing."]],
"WCH13/titration-to-find-concentration-solution-hydrochloric-acid":[
["Outline how to carry out the titration.","Pipette a fixed volume of the standard alkali into a conical flask, add indicator, and add acid from a burette until the end point. Repeat until titres are concordant."],
["What are concordant titres?","Titres within 0.10 cm³ of each other; the mean is taken from these."],
["Why is a conical flask rinsed with water but a burette rinsed with the solution it will hold?","Water in the flask does not change the moles of alkali. Water in the burette would dilute the acid and give a larger titre."],
["Name a suitable indicator and its colour change for a strong acid–strong base titration.","Phenolphthalein (pink in alkali to colourless) or methyl orange (yellow in alkali to orange at the end point)."],
["How is the acid concentration calculated?","Moles of alkali = c × V; use the equation ratio to get moles of acid; concentration = moles ÷ mean titre in dm³."]],
"WCH13/preparation-of-a-standard-solution-and-titration":[
["What is a standard solution?","A solution of accurately known concentration."],
["Outline how to make one from a solid.","Weigh the solid accurately, dissolve in distilled water in a beaker, transfer with washings to a volumetric flask, make up to the mark and invert several times to mix."],
["Why are the beaker and funnel rinsed into the flask?","To make sure all of the weighed solid is transferred."],
["How is the meniscus read?","With the bottom of the meniscus on the graduation line, viewed at eye level."],
["How do you calculate the percentage uncertainty in a titre?","(2 × burette uncertainty per reading ÷ titre) × 100, because two readings are taken."]],
"WCH13/rates-of-hydrolysis-of-halogenoalkanes":[
["Outline the method for comparing rates of hydrolysis.","Add each halogenoalkane to ethanol and warm in a water bath with separate tubes of aqueous silver nitrate. Mix and time how long a precipitate takes to appear."],
["Why is ethanol used?","It is a solvent in which both the halogenoalkane and the aqueous silver nitrate dissolve, so they mix."],
["What are the precipitate colours and the order of rate?","Chloro: white; bromo: cream; iodo: yellow. Iodoalkane is fastest, chloroalkane slowest."],
["Explain the order of reactivity.","The C–I bond has the lowest bond enthalpy, so it breaks most easily; C–Cl is the strongest."],
["How does structure (primary, secondary, tertiary) affect the rate?","Tertiary halogenoalkanes hydrolyse fastest and primary slowest."]],
"WCH13/chlorination-2-methylpropan-2-ol":[
["Which reagent converts 2-methylpropan-2-ol to 2-chloro-2-methylpropane?","Concentrated hydrochloric acid, shaken with the alcohol at room temperature."],
["Why is the mixture shaken in a separating funnel with the tap opened from time to time?","To mix the layers and release the pressure that builds up."],
["Why is the organic layer washed with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution?","To neutralise and remove any remaining acid."],
["How is the product dried and purified?","Add an anhydrous salt (e.g. sodium sulfate) until the liquid is clear, decant, then distil and collect the fraction at the product’s boiling temperature."],
["How can you tell which layer is the organic layer?","Add a little water: the layer that increases in volume is the aqueous one. The less dense organic layer is usually on top."]],
"WCH13/oxidation-propan-1-ol-propanol-propanoic-acid":[
["Which oxidising agent is used and what colour change is seen?","Potassium dichromate(VI) acidified with dilute sulfuric acid. Orange to green."],
["How are the conditions changed to get propanal or propanoic acid?","Distil the product off as it forms, with limited oxidising agent, for propanal. Heat under reflux with excess oxidising agent for propanoic acid."],
["Why does distilling give the aldehyde?","Propanal has a lower boiling temperature than the alcohol (no hydrogen bonding between its molecules), so it leaves the mixture before it can be oxidised further."],
["Why are anti-bumping granules added?","To give smooth boiling and prevent the liquid boiling violently."],
["How could you test the product to show it is an aldehyde and not an acid?","Warm with Tollens’ reagent (silver mirror) or Fehling’s solution (red precipitate); an acid would instead fizz with sodium carbonate."]],
"WCH13/analysis-of-some-inorganic-and-organic-unknowns":[
["How do you test for carbonate and sulfate ions?","Carbonate: add dilute acid; fizzing and the gas turns limewater cloudy. Sulfate: add dilute hydrochloric acid then barium chloride solution; white precipitate."],
["How do you test for halide ions?","Add dilute nitric acid then silver nitrate solution: chloride white, bromide cream, iodide yellow precipitate."],
["How do you test for ammonium ions?","Warm with sodium hydroxide solution; the gas given off turns damp red litmus paper blue."],
["How do you test for an alkene and for an alcohol?","Alkene: shake with bromine water; orange to colourless. Alcohol: warm with acidified potassium dichromate(VI); orange to green for primary and secondary (or add PCl₅: steamy fumes)."],
["Why is nitric acid added before silver nitrate in the halide test?","To remove ions such as carbonate that would also give a precipitate with silver ions."]],
"WCH16/rate-of-iodine-propanone-reaction":[
["Outline the titrimetric method.","Mix propanone, acid and iodine. At timed intervals remove samples, quench them with sodium hydrogencarbonate and titrate the remaining iodine with sodium thiosulfate using starch."],
["What does quenching do?","It neutralises the acid catalyst, which stops the reaction in that sample."],
["What does a straight-line graph of iodine concentration against time show?","The rate is constant, so the reaction is zero order with respect to iodine."],
["What does zero order in iodine tell you about the mechanism?","Iodine is not involved in the rate-determining step."],
["How can the reaction be followed without sampling?","By colorimetry, measuring the fading of the brown iodine colour."]],
"WCH16/finding-activation-energy-of-reaction":[
["Outline the method.","Carry out the same reaction at several temperatures and measure the time for a fixed change, such as a colour change, at each one."],
["What is plotted and how is Ea found?","ln(1/time), as a measure of ln rate, against 1/T in kelvin. Gradient = −Ea/R, so Ea = −gradient × 8.31."],
["Why can 1/time be used instead of rate?","The same amount of reaction happens each time, so rate is proportional to 1/time."],
["What must be kept the same in every run?","Concentrations and volumes of all the solutions."],
["How is the temperature of each run best recorded?","Take the temperature at the start and at the end and use the mean."]],
"WCH16/finding-ka-value-for-weak-acid":[
["Outline the half-neutralisation method.","Titrate a measured volume of the weak acid with sodium hydroxide to find the end-point volume. Then add exactly half that volume of alkali to a fresh sample and measure the pH."],
["Why does pH equal pKa at half-neutralisation?","Half the acid has been converted to its salt, so [HA] = [A⁻] and Ka = [H⁺]."],
["How is Ka calculated from the pH reading?","Ka = 10^(−pH)."],
["How should the pH meter be prepared?","Calibrate it with buffer solutions of known pH, and rinse the probe with distilled water between readings."],
["How else can Ka be found from a titration curve?","Read the pH at half the equivalence volume on a plotted pH curve."]],
"WCH16/investigating-electrochemical-cells":[
["How is an electrochemical cell set up?","Two half-cells (a metal dipping in a solution of its ions) joined by a salt bridge, with a high-resistance voltmeter connecting the metals."],
["What is the salt bridge made from and why?","Filter paper soaked in saturated potassium nitrate. Its ions carry charge between the solutions but do not react with them."],
["Why is a high-resistance voltmeter used?","So almost no current flows and the reading is the full e.m.f. of the cell."],
["Why are the metal electrodes cleaned with emery paper?","To remove the oxide layer so the metal is in contact with the solution."],
["Why might the measured e.m.f. differ from the data-book value?","Conditions are not standard: concentrations may not be 1 mol dm⁻³ and the temperature may not be 298 K."]],
"WCH16/carry-out-redox-titrations":[
["Outline a titration of iron(II) ions with potassium manganate(VII).","Pipette the iron(II) solution into a flask, add excess dilute sulfuric acid, and add manganate(VII) from the burette until the first permanent pale pink colour."],
["Write the ionic equation for the reaction.","MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 4H₂O + 5Fe³⁺."],
["Why is sulfuric acid used rather than hydrochloric acid?","Chloride ions would be oxidised by manganate(VII), giving a titre that is too high."],
["How is the burette read with potassium manganate(VII)?","From the top of the meniscus, because the dark colour hides the bottom."],
["In an iodine–thiosulfate titration, when is starch added and why then?","Near the end point, when the solution is pale yellow. Added earlier, the iodine binds strongly to the starch and the end point is not sharp."]],
"WCH16/preparation-transition-metal-complex":[
["What is the aim of this practical?","To prepare and isolate a pure, dry sample of a transition metal complex, for example a copper(II)–ammonia complex, from a metal salt."],
["How is the solid product separated and washed?","Filter under reduced pressure using a Buchner funnel, and wash with a small volume of cold solvent such as ethanol."],
["Why is ethanol often added to the reaction mixture?","The complex is less soluble in ethanol than in water, so it crystallises out."],
["How is percentage yield calculated?","(Actual mass of product ÷ theoretical mass from the limiting reagent) × 100."],
["Why should the work be done in a fume cupboard when concentrated ammonia is used?","Ammonia vapour is toxic and irritates the eyes and lungs."]],
"WCH16/analysis-of-some-inorganic-and-organic-unknowns":[
["What do sodium hydroxide and ammonia solutions show when added to transition metal ions?","Coloured hydroxide precipitates: Cu²⁺ blue, Fe²⁺ green, Fe³⁺ brown, Cr³⁺ green. With excess ammonia, the copper precipitate dissolves to a deep blue solution."],
["How do you distinguish an aldehyde from a ketone?","Warm with Tollens’ reagent or Fehling’s solution. Only the aldehyde gives a silver mirror or a red precipitate."],
["How do you test for a carbonyl group?","Add 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine; an orange precipitate forms."],
["How do you test for a carboxylic acid?","Add sodium carbonate or hydrogencarbonate; it fizzes, giving carbon dioxide."],
["What does a positive iodoform test look like and show?","A pale yellow precipitate with an antiseptic smell: a CH₃CO– group or CH₃CH(OH)– group is present."]],
"WCH16/preparation-of-aspirin":[
["Which reagents are used to make aspirin?","2-hydroxybenzoic acid (salicylic acid) and ethanoic anhydride, with a few drops of concentrated acid as catalyst, warmed gently."],
["Why is ethanoic anhydride preferred to ethanoyl chloride?","It is cheaper, less corrosive, reacts less violently with water and does not give off hydrogen chloride."],
["How is the crude aspirin purified?","Recrystallisation: dissolve in the minimum volume of hot solvent, cool, filter under reduced pressure, wash and dry."],
["Why is the minimum volume of hot solvent used?","So the solution is saturated and as much product as possible crystallises on cooling."],
["How is the purity of the aspirin checked?","Measure the melting temperature: a pure sample melts sharply at the data-book value. Thin-layer chromatography can also be used."]],
"WBI13/benedicts-test":[
["How is the Benedict’s test carried out?","Add Benedict’s solution to the sample and heat in a water bath."],
["What is a positive result?","The blue solution changes through green, yellow and orange to a brick-red precipitate, depending on how much reducing sugar is present."],
["How do you test for a non-reducing sugar such as sucrose?","After a negative test, boil a fresh sample with dilute hydrochloric acid, neutralise with sodium hydrogencarbonate, then repeat the Benedict’s test."],
["How can the test be made quantitative?","Make a calibration curve with glucose solutions of known concentration using a colorimeter, then read the unknown from the curve."],
["What must be kept the same for a fair comparison?","Volume of sample and of Benedict’s solution, temperature and heating time."]],
"WBI13/vitamin-c-content":[
["Which reagent is used to measure vitamin C and what is the end point?","DCPIP. The blue solution turns colourless when it is reduced by vitamin C."],
["Outline the method.","Add the juice drop by drop to a fixed volume of DCPIP, shaking gently, until the blue colour just disappears. Record the volume added."],
["How is the vitamin C concentration worked out?","First find the volume of a standard vitamin C solution of known concentration needed to decolourise the same volume of DCPIP, then compare."],
["Why should the tube be shaken gently and for the same time?","Vigorous shaking adds oxygen, which can re-oxidise the DCPIP and turn it blue again."],
["Give one limitation of the method.","The end point is hard to judge in coloured juices."]],
"WBI13/investigating-membrane-properties":[
["Why is beetroot used to investigate membrane permeability?","Its cells contain a red pigment (betalain) that leaks out when membranes are damaged."],
["Outline the method for the effect of temperature.","Cut equal-sized pieces, rinse, and place them in equal volumes of water at different temperatures for the same time. Remove the pieces and measure the absorbance of each solution with a colorimeter."],
["Why are the pieces rinsed first?","To remove pigment released from cells that were cut open."],
["Explain why more pigment leaks at high temperature.","Membrane proteins denature and the phospholipids move more and become more fluid, so the membrane becomes more permeable."],
["What effect does ethanol have, and why?","It increases permeability because it dissolves the lipids in the membrane."]],
"WBI13/enzyme-reactions":[
["What is meant by the initial rate of reaction, and why is it measured?","The rate at the very start, found from the gradient of the tangent at time zero. Substrate is not yet limiting, so results can be compared fairly."],
["Name two ways of following an enzyme reaction.","Measure the volume of gas produced (e.g. oxygen from catalase), or use a colorimeter to follow a colour change."],
["Which variables must be controlled when investigating enzyme concentration?","Temperature, pH (with a buffer), substrate concentration and the volumes used."],
["What does the graph of initial rate against enzyme concentration look like when substrate is in excess?","A straight line through the origin: rate is directly proportional to enzyme concentration."],
["Why is a buffer solution used?","To keep the pH constant, because pH changes alter the shape of the active site."]],
"WBI13/microscopy":[
["How do you calibrate an eyepiece graticule?","Line it up with a stage micrometer and work out how many micrometres one eyepiece division represents at that magnification."],
["Why must the graticule be recalibrated for each objective lens?","The size each division represents changes with the magnification."],
["State the equation linking magnification, image size and actual size.","Actual size = image size ÷ magnification."],
["Give three rules for a good biological drawing.","Use a sharp pencil with clear, continuous lines; no shading; label with ruled lines that do not cross; include a title and magnification or scale."],
["Why are thin specimens and stains used?","Thin sections let light pass through. Stains make particular structures visible by increasing contrast."]],
"WBI13/stages-mitosis":[
["Which part of a plant is used to observe mitosis, and why?","The root tip (e.g. of garlic or onion), because it contains a meristem where cells are dividing."],
["Why is the root tip treated with warm hydrochloric acid?","To break down the middle lamella holding the cells together, so they can be spread into a single layer."],
["Name a suitable stain and say what it does.","Acetic orcein or toluidine blue. It stains the chromosomes so they are visible."],
["Why is the tip squashed under the coverslip?","To spread the cells into one thin layer so light passes through and individual cells can be seen."],
["How is the mitotic index calculated?","Number of cells in mitosis ÷ total number of cells counted."]],
"WBI13/tensile-strength-plant-fibres":[
["How are fibres extracted from plant stems?","Soak the stems in water for about a week (retting) to soften the tissue, then pull out and dry the fibres."],
["Outline how tensile strength is measured.","Clamp a fibre of known length between two supports and add masses to its centre one at a time until it breaks. Record the breaking mass."],
["Which variables must be controlled?","Length and diameter of the fibre, the humidity and how the masses are added."],
["Why are repeats needed?","Fibres vary naturally, so repeats and a mean give a more reliable result."],
["Give one safety precaution.","Wear eye protection and keep feet clear of falling masses."]],
"WBI13/antimicrobial-properties-plants":[
["Outline how to test a plant extract for antimicrobial properties.","Spread bacteria evenly over an agar plate, place paper discs soaked in the extract on the surface, incubate, and measure the clear zones around the discs."],
["What does a clear zone show?","Bacteria have been killed or prevented from growing there. A wider zone means a more effective extract."],
["What control is needed?","A disc soaked only in the solvent used to make the extract, to show that the solvent is not responsible."],
["Give three aseptic techniques.","Flame the neck of the culture bottle, sterilise the spreader and forceps, work near a Bunsen flame, and lift the lid as little as possible."],
["Why are the plates incubated at about 25 °C and not sealed completely?","To avoid growing pathogens that thrive at body temperature, and to let oxygen in so harmful anaerobes do not grow."]],
"WBI16/factors-affecting-photosynthesis":[
["Outline a method for investigating light intensity and the rate of photosynthesis.","Place pondweed in water with sodium hydrogencarbonate and a lamp at a measured distance. Count bubbles or measure the volume of oxygen collected in a set time, then change the distance."],
["How is light intensity related to distance from the lamp?","Light intensity is proportional to 1/distance²."],
["Why is sodium hydrogencarbonate added?","To supply carbon dioxide so that it is not a limiting factor."],
["How is temperature kept constant?","Stand the tube in a water bath or put a heat shield (a tank of water) between the lamp and the plant."],
["Why is the plant left for a few minutes after each change?","To let it adjust to the new conditions so the rate is steady before measuring."]],
"WBI16/ecology-habitat":[
["When would you use random sampling and when a transect?","Random sampling for a uniform area. A transect when there is a gradient of conditions across the habitat."],
["How are quadrats placed at random?","Lay out a grid with tape measures and use a random number generator to give coordinates."],
["Name three ways of recording abundance in a quadrat.","Counting individuals (density), percentage cover, and frequency of occurrence."],
["Name three abiotic factors you might measure and the equipment used.","Light intensity (light meter), soil pH (pH probe), temperature (thermometer), soil moisture (moisture probe)."],
["Which statistical test shows whether abundance is correlated with an abiotic factor?","Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient."]],
"WBI16/temperature-development-organisms":[
["Outline a method to investigate temperature and the hatching of brine shrimp.","Put equal numbers of eggs into equal volumes of salt water at a range of temperatures. Count the number of hatched larvae each day."],
["Which variables must be controlled?","Salt concentration, volume, pH, light, oxygen supply and the number of eggs."],
["How can the larvae be counted easily?","Shine a bright light at one side; the larvae swim towards it and can be pipetted out and counted."],
["Why does the hatching rate rise with temperature and then fall?","Enzyme reactions speed up as temperature increases, but above the optimum the enzymes denature."],
["State one ethical consideration.","Treat living organisms with care, use only as many as needed, and return surviving animals to suitable conditions."]],
"WBI16/microorganisms-liquid-culture":[
["Name two ways to measure the growth of microorganisms in liquid culture.","Count cells with a haemocytometer, measure turbidity with a colorimeter, or do dilution plating."],
["What is a serial dilution and why is it done?","Repeatedly diluting the culture by a fixed factor, so the cells are few enough to count accurately."],
["How is turbidity related to cell number?","More cells scatter and absorb more light, so absorbance is higher. A calibration curve converts absorbance to cell number."],
["What is the difference between a total count and a viable count?","A total count includes living and dead cells; a viable count includes only living cells that can form colonies."],
["Name two factors to control when growing the culture.","Temperature, pH, nutrient concentration and oxygen supply (shake the flask)."]],
"WBI16/effect-different-antibodies":[
["Outline the method for comparing antibiotics.","Spread bacteria evenly on agar, place discs containing different antibiotics on the surface, incubate, and measure the diameter of the clear zones."],
["What is the clear area called and what does its size show?","The zone of inhibition. A larger zone means the bacteria are more sensitive to that antibiotic."],
["Why might there be no clear zone around a disc?","The bacteria are resistant to that antibiotic."],
["Why should the bacteria be spread as an even lawn?","So growth is uniform and the zones can be compared fairly."],
["How should the plates be disposed of?","Sterilise them in an autoclave before throwing them away."]],
"WBI16/respiration-yeast":[
["Outline a method for investigating the rate of respiration in yeast.","Mix a yeast suspension with a sugar solution at a set temperature and measure the volume of carbon dioxide produced in a fixed time, or the time for an indicator such as methylene blue to lose its colour."],
["Why does methylene blue turn colourless?","It accepts hydrogen removed during respiration and is reduced, so faster respiration decolourises it sooner."],
["Why is the mixture left to reach the bath temperature before timing?","So the yeast and its enzymes are at the temperature being tested from the start."],
["Which variables must be controlled?","Concentration and volume of yeast and of sugar, pH and time."],
["Why is a layer of oil sometimes added on top?","To keep oxygen out so that respiration is anaerobic."]],
"WBI16/rate-respiration-rq":[
["What does a respirometer measure?","The volume of oxygen taken up by living organisms in a given time."],
["What is the soda lime (or potassium hydroxide) for?","It absorbs the carbon dioxide produced, so the movement of the liquid shows only oxygen uptake."],
["Why is a control tube with glass beads needed?","To correct for changes in volume caused by temperature or pressure changes."],
["How is the volume of oxygen calculated?","Distance moved by the liquid × cross-sectional area of the capillary tube (πr²)."],
["How can the respirometer be used to find RQ?","Repeat without the carbon dioxide absorber to find the net volume change, work out the CO₂ produced, and divide by the oxygen used."]],
"WBI16/effects-exercise":[
["What does a spirometer trace show?","Changes in lung volume with time, from which tidal volume, breathing rate and oxygen consumption can be found."],
["How are tidal volume and breathing rate read from the trace?","Tidal volume: the height of one peak above its trough at rest. Breathing rate: the number of peaks per minute."],
["How is minute ventilation calculated?","Tidal volume × breathing rate."],
["How is oxygen consumption found from the trace?","From the downward slope of the trace: the fall in volume per unit time, as oxygen is used and carbon dioxide is absorbed by soda lime."],
["State two safety precautions.","Use a sterilised mouthpiece, fresh soda lime, medical-grade oxygen, and stop if the person feels unwell."]],
"WBI16/production-amylase-cereal-grains":[
["What triggers amylase production in a germinating cereal grain?","The embryo releases gibberellin, which makes the aleurone layer produce amylase."],
["How is amylase activity detected on a starch agar plate?","Flood the plate with iodine solution. A clear zone where starch has been digested appears around the seed against a blue-black background."],
["Why are the seeds cut in half and only some halves contain the embryo?","To show that without the embryo no gibberellin is made, so no amylase is produced unless gibberellin is added."],
["How does gibberellin switch on amylase production?","It causes the genes for amylase to be transcribed, by removing a repressor of the transcription factor."],
["Why are the seeds surface-sterilised first?","To kill microorganisms that would also digest the starch."]]};
